00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:28.690
and for your work pulling
today's event together.

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:31.390
And I want to just
use a minute to thank

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.150
Beth and her amazing team.

00:07:34.150 --> 00:07:36.580
She talks about how I wanted
to three or four events

00:07:36.580 --> 00:07:38.080
a night during reunion weekend.

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.460
It would be
absolutely impossible

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.252
without this crackerjack
team that actually

00:07:41.252 --> 00:07:42.790
does create schedules
for me that I

00:07:42.790 --> 00:07:44.630
describe as a jigsaw puzzle.

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:46.600
The events are knit
together like this.

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:48.610
So I know I saw many
of you last night

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.710
and at your fantastic events.

00:07:50.710 --> 00:07:52.840
The mood is among you--

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:54.560
I think, reflects
the mood on campus,

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.230
which is optimistic, ebullient.

00:07:58.230 --> 00:08:00.370
I'm very much excited
about what MIT

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:01.900
is doing on campus
and frankly, what

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:05.720
we contribute to the world.

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.940
Tech Day is always one
of the highlights for me

00:08:09.940 --> 00:08:12.880
and partly because I get
to see so many of you

00:08:12.880 --> 00:08:17.110
but also because we get to
hear from MIT's faculty leaders

00:08:17.110 --> 00:08:18.910
about the work
that they're doing

00:08:18.910 --> 00:08:22.120
and how the Institute
addresses the pressing

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:23.910
challenges of the world.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:27.850
Today, we're going to focus
on what many people consider--

00:08:27.850 --> 00:08:29.410
and I'm among them--

00:08:29.410 --> 00:08:32.720
perhaps the world's greatest
challenge right now.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:35.799
It's a challenge-- energy,
sustainable energy,

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:42.190
how we can use it without using
up our important resources.

00:08:42.190 --> 00:08:44.650
Those resources include the
capacity of the atmosphere

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:46.810
to take up CO2.

00:08:46.810 --> 00:08:50.710
It's also an area
where we believe MIT

00:08:50.710 --> 00:08:53.080
can make unique contributions.

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:55.990
Now while I'm going to provide
a little bit of an introduction

00:08:55.990 --> 00:08:58.510
to the energy talks that
you're going to be hearing,

00:08:58.510 --> 00:09:00.010
we will have Q&amp;A at the end.

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:03.690
And I welcome
questions on any topic.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:06.450
Now, interestingly
enough, the best way

00:09:06.450 --> 00:09:11.220
to think about
MIT's work on energy

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.360
is to begin not here in
Cambridge, but in Switzerland

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:20.320
at this year's World Economic
Forum in Davos last January.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:23.790
At Davos this year, we had
a very strong MIT presence.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:27.150
We had more than a dozen faculty
members who accompanied me.

00:09:27.150 --> 00:09:28.860
Over the course of
several days, we

00:09:28.860 --> 00:09:32.510
had 17 faculty presentations
at the World Economic Forum.

00:09:32.510 --> 00:09:36.310
And there were several MIT
events in Zurich and at Davos,

00:09:36.310 --> 00:09:39.990
including a dinner that we
hosted on disruptive energy

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:40.950
technologies.

00:09:44.450 --> 00:09:46.260
We were agreed with
enormous enthusiasm,

00:09:46.260 --> 00:09:48.930
and rather than brag
about it myself,

00:09:48.930 --> 00:09:53.370
I thought I'd just read from you
from what the Time Magazine's

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.760
Davos correspondent wrote.

00:09:56.760 --> 00:10:00.030
At a certain point during this
year's World Economic Forum

00:10:00.030 --> 00:10:03.450
annual meeting in Davos,
the gusts of self-regard

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:05.880
became a little
too much to bear.

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:08.880
I found myself wondering whether
any of this high minded talk

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:10.560
was worth a dime.

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:12.690
Fortunately, that's when
something came along

00:10:12.690 --> 00:10:15.210
to remind me of what
Davos is good for.

00:10:15.210 --> 00:10:16.980
My breakthrough
session turned out

00:10:16.980 --> 00:10:19.220
to be a guided tour
of the future--

00:10:19.220 --> 00:10:23.570
MIT's dinner session on
disruptive energy technologies.

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:26.700
Now at the dinner we had
three of our faculty speak.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.040
Angie Belcher talked
about the viruses

00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.530
that she's persuaded
to make batteries.

00:10:31.530 --> 00:10:36.390
Vladimir Bulovic talked about
solar photovoltaics and quantum

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:37.460
dots.

00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:41.940
Greg Stephanopoulos talked about
a very large and fascinating

00:10:41.940 --> 00:10:43.880
biofuels project.

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:46.380
And Tom Friedman who you know
from his work for the New York

00:10:46.380 --> 00:10:51.450
Times was our introducer and
moderated the discussion.

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:55.740
Back to Time Magazine-- no
wonder MIT President Susan

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:58.440
Hockfield, seeing those sorts
of discoveries taking place

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:02.190
in nooks and crannies all around
her Cambridge campus decided

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:04.890
to gather scientists into
an Energy Research Council

00:11:04.890 --> 00:11:07.320
and declare war on
the energy crisis,

00:11:07.320 --> 00:11:10.530
accelerating the switch
to clean, efficient fuels.

00:11:10.530 --> 00:11:12.570
Solving this crisis,
as Hockfield told us

00:11:12.570 --> 00:11:16.730
as candles flickered in the
little stone grotto in Davos,

00:11:16.730 --> 00:11:20.670
will take not one solution,
but a portfolio of solutions.

00:11:20.670 --> 00:11:23.377
It's a sprint and a marathon.

00:11:23.377 --> 00:11:24.960
Learning what her
scientists are up to

00:11:24.960 --> 00:11:27.700
made me feel better about
our chances in this race.

00:11:27.700 --> 00:11:29.760
And that's what
Davos is good for--

00:11:29.760 --> 00:11:32.310
helping of authentic
reality-based

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.820
hope right when you
need it the most.

00:11:35.820 --> 00:11:40.530
And that is where I begin
what MIT is good for--

00:11:40.530 --> 00:11:42.570
a dose of authentic
reality-based

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:45.930
hope that we can help
address in a real way

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:49.068
the most serious of the
world's great challenges.

00:11:49.068 --> 00:11:50.610
Now I'm sure I don't
need to rehearse

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:55.110
for this audience what actually
frames our global energy

00:11:55.110 --> 00:11:56.340
challenge.

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.680
There is accelerating demand.

00:11:58.680 --> 00:12:02.850
China alone is increasing
its energy use by 10% a year.

00:12:02.850 --> 00:12:08.190
The world over is on track to
double our energy use by 2050.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:10.230
The security of our
sources of energy

00:12:10.230 --> 00:12:13.420
are historical
sources of energy are,

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:15.630
shall we say, not so stable.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.940
Increasingly, those
energy sources--

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:20.190
the energy there itself, oil--

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:24.660
is in the hands of companies
that are controlled by states.

00:12:24.660 --> 00:12:26.580
Those states are,
shall we say, can't

00:12:26.580 --> 00:12:30.360
be relied upon to be the
most friendly to our country.

00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:32.310
And then the
environmental implications

00:12:32.310 --> 00:12:35.040
of our current technology--

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:39.720
people may argue about the cause
of about whether the increased

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.460
CO2 in the atmosphere is
the cause of global warming.

00:12:42.460 --> 00:12:44.970
I personally believe what the
preponderance of scientists

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:46.148
say about this.

00:12:46.148 --> 00:12:47.940
But if you look at the
concentration of CO2

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:51.240
in the atmosphere, that was
about 280 parts per million

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:52.350
in 1900.

00:12:52.350 --> 00:12:54.840
It is 380 parts
per million today.

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.350
It is rising at a rate that
is absolutely unsustainable.

00:12:58.350 --> 00:13:01.530
Whether that is the cause
of global warming or no,

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.850
we are using up a
limited resource,

00:13:03.850 --> 00:13:06.000
and that limited
resource is the capacity

00:13:06.000 --> 00:13:09.780
of our atmosphere to hold CO2
in a way that is sustainable.

00:13:09.780 --> 00:13:15.190
It's consistent with life as
we know it on this planet.

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:17.730
MIT's response to
this energy challenge

00:13:17.730 --> 00:13:20.010
is the MIT Energy Initiative.

00:13:20.010 --> 00:13:22.290
The initiative grew
out of interest

00:13:22.290 --> 00:13:26.580
I heard from faculty,
students, staff, and alumni

00:13:26.580 --> 00:13:29.220
as I was learning about
the MIT community.

00:13:29.220 --> 00:13:32.060
When I asked our
community, what are

00:13:32.060 --> 00:13:34.680
MIT's responsibilities
and opportunities

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:36.480
over the next decade or two?

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:38.550
There was an
overwhelming call for MIT

00:13:38.550 --> 00:13:42.780
to do more to help solve
this global crisis.

00:13:42.780 --> 00:13:46.410
The Energy Research
Council released its report

00:13:46.410 --> 00:13:48.990
after a year of study
just about a year ago.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:51.990
It was released concurrent
with an Institute-wide energy

00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:56.100
forum that was about the
busiest week of the year.

00:13:56.100 --> 00:13:57.180
We held it in Kresge.

00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:00.170
Kresge was packed.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:05.390
This past fall, we announced the
formal begin of the MIT Energy

00:14:05.390 --> 00:14:06.290
Initiative.

00:14:06.290 --> 00:14:09.320
The initiative is
headed by Ernie Moniz

00:14:09.320 --> 00:14:12.840
with the deputy
director Barb Armstrong.

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:18.160
One of the most exciting things
about MIT's Energy Initiative

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:21.810
is not the huge number of people
who have begun to participate,

00:14:21.810 --> 00:14:24.120
but the fact that people
are participating in this

00:14:24.120 --> 00:14:26.850
from all five of our schools.

00:14:26.850 --> 00:14:30.670
The Energy Initiative has
drawn out three main themes.

00:14:30.670 --> 00:14:34.170
The first is new technologies
for a cleaner future,

00:14:34.170 --> 00:14:38.220
and there, we're focusing
on solar, on biofuels,

00:14:38.220 --> 00:14:40.630
and on energy storage.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:43.260
The second theme is improving
today's energy systems

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:45.480
for changes in the near
term, and there we're

00:14:45.480 --> 00:14:49.740
focusing on nuclear, clean
carbon, and carbon dioxide

00:14:49.740 --> 00:14:52.470
capture and sequestration.

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:55.050
The third theme is addressing
the challenges posed

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:57.220
by the emerging economies.

00:14:57.220 --> 00:14:59.580
And there we feel we can
be particularly helpful

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:03.030
in the areas of transportation,
and construction.

00:15:03.030 --> 00:15:04.770
And our three faculty
speakers today

00:15:04.770 --> 00:15:06.900
will lead you through a few--

00:15:06.900 --> 00:15:10.620
let's just say three of the
very many important strands that

00:15:10.620 --> 00:15:14.610
comprise the Energy Initiative.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:18.300
As I said, an important aspect
of the Energy Initiative

00:15:18.300 --> 00:15:21.390
is that it reaches across
all of the schools.

00:15:21.390 --> 00:15:24.300
Every theme, every
research direction

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.450
requires collaboration from
many different departments

00:15:27.450 --> 00:15:29.220
in many different schools.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:33.060
Each research area that I
hear described by the Energy

00:15:33.060 --> 00:15:36.210
Initiative participants
is not an area

00:15:36.210 --> 00:15:39.330
that could be addressed by
one faculty member or one

00:15:39.330 --> 00:15:41.010
department or one school.

00:15:41.010 --> 00:15:44.670
Each requires
participation across MIT,

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:47.310
and each also requires
not simply new technology

00:15:47.310 --> 00:15:48.360
or new science.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:52.770
Each requires technology
and policy working together.

00:15:52.770 --> 00:15:56.460
The Energy Initiative is
not just about research.

00:15:56.460 --> 00:16:00.420
Importantly, it's
also about education.

00:16:00.420 --> 00:16:03.300
There is enormous student
enthusiasm and interest,

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:06.660
and one of the first activities
of the Energy Initiative

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:09.990
was to put together a
task force on education.

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:12.400
We have a marvelous committee
that's addressing that,

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:14.880
and we now have a website
that presents to our students

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:18.970
the range of energy-related
courses available on campus.

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:20.470
And the student
interest, as I said,

00:16:20.470 --> 00:16:22.680
has just been overwhelming.

00:16:22.680 --> 00:16:26.860
It's also important for MIT
to, what we say, walk the talk.

00:16:26.860 --> 00:16:29.220
So one of the initiatives
first task force

00:16:29.220 --> 00:16:32.160
is a task force on
campus sustainability

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.220
to use our campus as a
living laboratory, so we

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.620
can do what we're talking
about and what we're urging

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:39.510
the rest of the world to do.

00:16:39.510 --> 00:16:42.840
The most important message
emerging out of our work

00:16:42.840 --> 00:16:46.110
is that we have to develop
a portfolio of solutions.

00:16:46.110 --> 00:16:49.240
There is not a
single silver bullet.

00:16:49.240 --> 00:16:52.200
We can't focus on one
solution to address it.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:56.610
We have to at the same time
pursue many different avenues.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:59.465
When I first heard about
the energy challenge,

00:16:59.465 --> 00:17:00.840
I thought about
it as a challenge

00:17:00.840 --> 00:17:02.010
and you know with
all seriousness

00:17:02.010 --> 00:17:04.552
about wearing extra sweaters
and turning down the thermostat.

00:17:04.552 --> 00:17:09.150
And it seemed like, boy, it
was kind of a sorry thing.

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:12.270
After talking to our faculty
being in my own energy course

00:17:12.270 --> 00:17:14.910
from our faculty and students
over the last two years,

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:18.240
I now see this as a fantastic
opportunity-- an opportunity

00:17:18.240 --> 00:17:21.420
not just for a campus, but an
opportunity for the nation.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.810
It's an opportunity to inspire
the young people of the nation

00:17:24.810 --> 00:17:28.200
around a bright future
that they can help invent.

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:29.880
I see this as a
possibility of having

00:17:29.880 --> 00:17:33.330
the same kind of catalytic
and inspirational effect

00:17:33.330 --> 00:17:36.810
that the Apollo program
had on my generation.

00:17:36.810 --> 00:17:39.690
The other great opportunity
is the possibility

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:42.870
that new energy
technology will fuel

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:46.200
the next cycle of the
innovation economy

00:17:46.200 --> 00:17:48.340
that has made this
nation so strong.

00:17:48.340 --> 00:17:50.340
We see that around here
where energy startups

00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:51.840
and actually, large
energy companies

00:17:51.840 --> 00:17:54.450
are moving into MIT's backyard.

00:17:54.450 --> 00:17:57.030
It resembles, in many ways, the
beginning of the life science

00:17:57.030 --> 00:17:59.190
revolution and the
marvelous life science

00:17:59.190 --> 00:18:02.370
companies that have come
out of places like MIT

00:18:02.370 --> 00:18:04.320
around the world.

00:18:04.320 --> 00:18:06.360
Now, of course, energy
is not the only way

00:18:06.360 --> 00:18:09.210
in which MIT's faculty,
students, and staff are working

00:18:09.210 --> 00:18:11.730
together to address the
world's most critical needs,

00:18:11.730 --> 00:18:14.190
and we could have picked any
one of a number of topics

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:17.300
to present to you today.

00:18:17.300 --> 00:18:19.840
But I would say,
overall, there is

00:18:19.840 --> 00:18:22.630
a huge sense of
optimism and opportunity

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:27.940
on campus about what
MIT is good for--

00:18:27.940 --> 00:18:29.950
a dose of authentic
reality-based

00:18:29.950 --> 00:18:32.260
hope that we can
contribute to solving

00:18:32.260 --> 00:18:35.410
the world's great challenges.

00:18:35.410 --> 00:18:39.610
In this role, MIT has been
for decades and decades

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:42.220
a real beacon for the world.

00:18:42.220 --> 00:18:46.570
Now thanks to an
extraordinarily generous alumnus

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:49.360
who is inspired by
the idea of taking

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:54.040
the basket off this beacon,
off the lamp, tonight,

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:57.880
we're going to celebrate
a symbolic way of taking

00:18:57.880 --> 00:18:59.500
the basket off the lamp.

00:18:59.500 --> 00:19:04.150
And we will reignite one of my
team's most powerful symbols.

00:19:04.150 --> 00:19:06.070
After sunset tonight,
which occurs late

00:19:06.070 --> 00:19:09.010
because we are near the
longest day of the year,

00:19:09.010 --> 00:19:11.530
for the first time in
more than three decades,

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:14.880
we will fully relight
the great dome.

00:19:14.880 --> 00:19:17.770
The relighting is possible
because of advances

00:19:17.770 --> 00:19:20.500
in energy-saving
lighting technologies.

00:19:20.500 --> 00:19:24.970
The power consumption of
our new lighting techniques

00:19:24.970 --> 00:19:26.380
is going to consume
energy that's

00:19:26.380 --> 00:19:28.602
equal to two hair
dryers, or I've

00:19:28.602 --> 00:19:31.060
been told the amount of energy
that's currently is consumed

00:19:31.060 --> 00:19:32.940
by lighting in my office.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:35.190
We've got to get to work on
the lighting in my office.

00:19:38.860 --> 00:19:42.160
So the lighting itself is
very low energy consuming.

00:19:42.160 --> 00:19:45.340
But in addition to that, again,
because of the generosity

00:19:45.340 --> 00:19:50.990
of this alumnus who has
insisted on remaining anonymous,

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:52.720
we are also going
to be installing

00:19:52.720 --> 00:19:55.540
40-kilowatt photovoltaic
cell, actually,

00:19:55.540 --> 00:20:02.590
which greatly exceeds the call
on energy by the dome lighting.

00:20:02.590 --> 00:20:05.530
But we see this dome
lighting as a way

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:08.290
to walk the talk times two.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:11.800
The first is demonstrating
energy efficient lighting.

00:20:11.800 --> 00:20:15.010
We don't have to give up a
lot of the wonderful things

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:15.610
we enjoy.

00:20:15.610 --> 00:20:19.150
We just have to do things in
a more energy-efficient way.

00:20:19.150 --> 00:20:22.630
And the second really important
part of MIT's contributions

00:20:22.630 --> 00:20:23.420
is inspiring.

00:20:23.420 --> 00:20:25.900
The nation the world about
the wonderful promise

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:29.860
of a brighter future for all
through Science and Technology.

00:20:29.860 --> 00:20:32.410
Let me just close by
echoing some of the things

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:34.300
that Beth said
when she introduced

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:35.800
the sessions morning.

00:20:35.800 --> 00:20:39.070
I cannot overestimate
the importance of alumni

00:20:39.070 --> 00:20:40.840
engagement.

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:42.580
Of course, you should
go without saying

00:20:42.580 --> 00:20:45.040
that your generous financial
support is essential for us

00:20:45.040 --> 00:20:48.280
to be able to do the kind
of extraordinary frontier

00:20:48.280 --> 00:20:50.500
advancing work that
we do and to bring

00:20:50.500 --> 00:20:53.110
the most able and ambitious
students in the world

00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:54.337
to this campus.

00:20:54.337 --> 00:20:56.170
And I'm going to thank
you a little bit more

00:20:56.170 --> 00:20:58.390
in that domain at lunch.

00:20:58.390 --> 00:21:00.880
But for now, I really
want to thank you

00:21:00.880 --> 00:21:04.300
for the very many other roles
you play as members of the MIT

00:21:04.300 --> 00:21:05.920
community.

00:21:05.920 --> 00:21:09.520
One especially critical role
is as ambassadors from MIT

00:21:09.520 --> 00:21:10.760
to the world.

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:12.910
And I very much
hope that all of you

00:21:12.910 --> 00:21:17.380
will continue to spread the
good news of MIT's contributions

00:21:17.380 --> 00:21:19.690
to solving the world's
great challenges

00:21:19.690 --> 00:21:23.530
and how we educate
young people from MIT

00:21:23.530 --> 00:21:27.400
to be the people who engineer,
who develop the science, who

00:21:27.400 --> 00:21:29.260
understand the
policy of bringing

00:21:29.260 --> 00:21:32.540
those solutions to bear
on these great challenges.

00:21:32.540 --> 00:21:35.480
Thank you very much for joining
us for this reunion weekend.

00:21:35.480 --> 00:21:38.500
Let me stop there and
take a few questions

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:40.670
before we move on to our
faculty presentations.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:41.401
Thank you.

00:21:41.401 --> 00:21:48.135
[APPLAUSE]

00:21:52.950 --> 00:21:56.808
Any questions from
the floor before we--

00:21:56.808 --> 00:21:58.200
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:58.200 --> 00:21:59.767
HOCKFIELD: Yes, there are mics.

00:21:59.767 --> 00:22:00.600
Oh, you're up there.

00:22:00.600 --> 00:22:05.128
Okay, so you'll have to
speak very loudly, please.

00:22:05.128 --> 00:22:09.511
AUDIENCE: Obviously, [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:24.200 --> 00:22:26.430
HOCKFIELD: Yeah so
the question is,

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:29.100
it's obvious that MIT can make
major contributions in science

00:22:29.100 --> 00:22:30.210
and technology.

00:22:30.210 --> 00:22:33.060
But are we also working on
the policy, the economic piece

00:22:33.060 --> 00:22:33.990
of the puzzle?

00:22:33.990 --> 00:22:36.060
And indeed, we are.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:38.070
The Energy Council, which

00:22:38.070 --> 00:22:41.220
is the five faculty who
are primary advisors

00:22:41.220 --> 00:22:44.160
to Ernie Moniz and
Bob Armstrong come

00:22:44.160 --> 00:22:46.290
from each of the five schools.

00:22:46.290 --> 00:22:49.030
And we have a number of
faculty in humanities, arts,

00:22:49.030 --> 00:22:50.970
and social sciences
and MIT Sloan who

00:22:50.970 --> 00:22:53.553
are actually very interested in
these policy, economic issues.

00:22:53.553 --> 00:22:55.762
And I think you'll be hearing
a little bit about some

00:22:55.762 --> 00:22:57.210
of that today looking at Nazli.

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:58.680
And she's not nodding.

00:22:58.680 --> 00:23:01.347
We're going to hear a little bit
about some of their policy work

00:23:01.347 --> 00:23:02.100
today.

00:23:02.100 --> 00:23:04.980
And we actually feel that
for a couple of reasons

00:23:04.980 --> 00:23:09.630
that MIT can knit the science
and technology together

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:11.980
with the policy in
almost unique ways,

00:23:11.980 --> 00:23:14.280
partly because we have
people on campus who

00:23:14.280 --> 00:23:17.790
are working on both
sides of that equation,

00:23:17.790 --> 00:23:22.060
but also because MIT has
an almost unique practice

00:23:22.060 --> 00:23:26.220
of faculty working across
what in other places

00:23:26.220 --> 00:23:28.740
are departmental and
school boundaries.

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:29.770
As I think about--

00:23:29.770 --> 00:23:33.180
at least the parts of
MIT's history that I know--

00:23:33.180 --> 00:23:36.540
what I always think
about is the study

00:23:36.540 --> 00:23:40.080
that led to the book Made in
America that was brought forth

00:23:40.080 --> 00:23:43.770
during the Japanese threat
on the American economy.

00:23:43.770 --> 00:23:45.660
Made in America was
done by a group called

00:23:45.660 --> 00:23:47.370
the industrial
Performance Center,

00:23:47.370 --> 00:23:50.100
which is a group of
economists, people

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:52.530
from MIT Sloan,
engineers and scientists.

00:23:52.530 --> 00:23:55.260
That group continues to produce
a bunch of a number of books

00:23:55.260 --> 00:23:57.900
and actually their most
recent book out of that group.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:23:59.873
The main author
is Suzanne Berger.

00:23:59.873 --> 00:24:01.290
It's a book called
How We Compete,

00:24:01.290 --> 00:24:03.165
and it's exactly just
exactly what you asked.

00:24:03.165 --> 00:24:06.600
It's a study of
economics and business

00:24:06.600 --> 00:24:10.500
practices in the context of
globally successful businesses.

00:24:10.500 --> 00:24:12.720
So indeed, the short
answer I should've said

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:15.690
is, yes, we are going
to be we are doing

00:24:15.690 --> 00:24:17.790
both the economics
and the technology

00:24:17.790 --> 00:24:19.680
around the energy challenges.

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:21.005
Other questions?

00:24:21.005 --> 00:24:23.380
You'll have to actually speak
up because it's hard for me

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:25.303
to see if there's
anyone at a microphone.

00:24:28.690 --> 00:24:31.190
If not, we will
proceed with the--

00:24:31.190 --> 00:24:33.486
oh, thank you.

00:24:33.486 --> 00:24:36.937
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:42.860 --> 00:24:45.740
HOCKFIELD: What can we do
to persuade our government

00:24:45.740 --> 00:24:49.220
leaders that we need programs
like Apollo in this area?

00:24:49.220 --> 00:24:52.700
Well, I think we all can
lend our voice to that call.

00:24:52.700 --> 00:24:55.670
Like MIT presidents
before me, I spend a day

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:58.130
every four to six
weeks in Washington,

00:24:58.130 --> 00:25:01.140
working to persuade them
actually on these issues.

00:25:01.140 --> 00:25:04.430
I spent time in Congress
at the White House

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:06.890
with the heads of
the various agencies

00:25:06.890 --> 00:25:08.480
actually making this call.

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.390
And I think we
need to as a nation

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:17.090
articulate our interest in a
very loud way on our belief

00:25:17.090 --> 00:25:20.570
that the energy
challenge could represent

00:25:20.570 --> 00:25:23.420
the kind of issue to which
the nation as a whole

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:27.110
could direct its interest,
its enthusiasm, its ambitions.

00:25:27.110 --> 00:25:31.010
So as I said, you are very
important as MIT ambassadors

00:25:31.010 --> 00:25:31.630
to the world.

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:34.130
If you all lend your
voice to this possibility,

00:25:34.130 --> 00:25:40.817
it will amplify the things that
I can do in person 120,000-fold

00:25:40.817 --> 00:25:42.650
I think is a number of
our alumni right now.

00:25:47.490 --> 00:25:48.240
Anything else?

00:25:48.240 --> 00:25:50.782
Well, again, thank you very much
for joining us for Tech Day.

00:25:50.782 --> 00:25:52.210
Thanks for coming
back to campus,

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:53.790
I hope you like what you see.

00:25:53.790 --> 00:25:56.640
And as Beth said, this
is not a shy group.

00:25:56.640 --> 00:25:59.760
We look forward to hearing
from you about your reflections

00:25:59.760 --> 00:26:01.090
on this week on campus.

00:26:01.090 --> 00:26:02.150
Welcome to MIT.

00:26:02.150 --> 00:26:08.650
[APPLAUSE]

00:26:16.863 --> 00:26:18.530
HOCHMAN: Thank you,
President Hockfield,

00:26:18.530 --> 00:26:22.280
for those very interesting
information, particularly

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:25.520
hearing about the exciting
research and implementation

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:29.450
going on at MIT transporting
us to Switzerland

00:26:29.450 --> 00:26:31.760
and also for sending us
on edge for the words

00:26:31.760 --> 00:26:33.840
of our next speaker.

00:26:33.840 --> 00:26:37.100
There are detailed biographies
of the speakers in your program

00:26:37.100 --> 00:26:38.660
books, but I would
like to highlight

00:26:38.660 --> 00:26:44.300
some facets of our next speaker,
Professor Nazli Choucri.

00:26:44.300 --> 00:26:47.300
In addition to being a professor
at MIT's political science

00:26:47.300 --> 00:26:49.730
department, she
is also associated

00:26:49.730 --> 00:26:53.150
director of the technology
and development program

00:26:53.150 --> 00:26:57.230
and head of the Middle
East program at MIT.

00:26:57.230 --> 00:26:59.990
Professor Choucri's
primary research focus

00:26:59.990 --> 00:27:03.620
is in international relations
and international political

00:27:03.620 --> 00:27:08.330
economy with a special focus
on conflict, connectivity,

00:27:08.330 --> 00:27:10.460
and the global environment.

00:27:10.460 --> 00:27:13.610
This puts her in a particularly
advantageous perspective

00:27:13.610 --> 00:27:15.860
to comment on the
broader context

00:27:15.860 --> 00:27:19.320
of the energy challenges we
now face-- political, economic,

00:27:19.320 --> 00:27:22.970
and strategic, as well as
the types of coordination

00:27:22.970 --> 00:27:24.890
that the major
nations of the world

00:27:24.890 --> 00:27:28.640
will need to develop to
ensure our energy future.

00:27:28.640 --> 00:27:31.310
I'd also like to mention
that there are microphones

00:27:31.310 --> 00:27:32.370
located in the aisles.

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:34.430
If you have
questions, please feel

00:27:34.430 --> 00:27:37.160
free to line up at
the microphones.

00:27:37.160 --> 00:27:40.910
So please join me in welcoming
Professor Nazli Choucri.

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:47.090
[APPLAUSE]

00:27:54.020 --> 00:27:55.820
CHOUCRI: Thank you,
Ira, for this very

00:27:55.820 --> 00:27:58.270
gracious introduction.

00:27:58.270 --> 00:28:02.480
I'm going to ask you to
work with me as we place

00:28:02.480 --> 00:28:06.040
energy in a global context.

00:28:06.040 --> 00:28:09.020
And part of what I will
say will not seem obvious

00:28:09.020 --> 00:28:10.748
as to what the energy
connections are,

00:28:10.748 --> 00:28:12.290
and this is where
your homework comes

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:14.960
in helping me out on this.

00:28:18.760 --> 00:28:21.380
So am I doing this right.

00:28:21.380 --> 00:28:21.880
Hmm.

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:30.160
This is not my machine.

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:33.010
I have no clue how
this thing works.

00:28:33.010 --> 00:28:36.170
Okay we'll try again.

00:28:36.170 --> 00:28:37.850
Ah, it's this way?

00:28:37.850 --> 00:28:39.040
HOCHMAN: It's it right here.

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:39.623
CHOUCRI: Yeah.

00:28:42.260 --> 00:28:43.800
Good.

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:45.000
This is the social sciences.

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:53.890
I'm going to be talking
about three issues,

00:28:53.890 --> 00:28:56.930
and each one of them is
really quite important.

00:28:56.930 --> 00:29:02.420
The first is to take stock of
the legacy of the 20th century.

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:05.470
What is it that we're
carrying with us today?

00:29:05.470 --> 00:29:09.390
The second is to look at
the global system as a whole

00:29:09.390 --> 00:29:15.380
and in what I will refer to as
some odd views of the system

00:29:15.380 --> 00:29:19.600
and finally, some political
realities for energy

00:29:19.600 --> 00:29:21.890
in the 21st century.

00:29:21.890 --> 00:29:25.430
So we'll take those
one at a time.

00:29:25.430 --> 00:29:31.130
On the legacies, I've chosen
to focus on four core ones

00:29:31.130 --> 00:29:32.870
and then look at some
of the surprises--

00:29:32.870 --> 00:29:37.490
the good news, the bad news,
and the unexpected from which I

00:29:37.490 --> 00:29:38.160
will ask.

00:29:38.160 --> 00:29:40.633
So I will propose
the balance sheet.

00:29:40.633 --> 00:29:41.300
What do we have?

00:29:41.300 --> 00:29:42.425
What do we get out of this?

00:29:42.425 --> 00:29:44.690
What are the net effects?

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:49.730
So on the legacies, the
ones that I have chosen

00:29:49.730 --> 00:29:53.910
are self-evident, but there are
surprises in each one of them.

00:29:53.910 --> 00:29:55.490
The first is
population dynamics,

00:29:55.490 --> 00:29:58.070
and we're all familiar with
the rabbit population growth

00:29:58.070 --> 00:30:00.050
almost in one word.

00:30:00.050 --> 00:30:02.510
Then the issue of
some issues related

00:30:02.510 --> 00:30:06.780
to technology and
implications for energy use.

00:30:06.780 --> 00:30:09.990
Some energy basics in the
really most basic sense

00:30:09.990 --> 00:30:12.360
is seen from a
political scientist.

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:17.100
And then a reminder, the fourth
legacy is a reminder of the new

00:30:17.100 --> 00:30:20.830
global agenda-- the new
global political agenda--

00:30:20.830 --> 00:30:24.330
the world out there that
is changing, perhaps

00:30:24.330 --> 00:30:28.620
far more rapidly than we
are able to understand

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:30.560
or accommodate it.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:33.510
So one legacy at a time--

00:30:33.510 --> 00:30:36.300
on population dynamics,
we all know that there

00:30:36.300 --> 00:30:38.700
are more people on this planet.

00:30:38.700 --> 00:30:42.360
We also know that there's some
important demographic shifts.

00:30:42.360 --> 00:30:44.380
We're not exactly sure
what the shifts are

00:30:44.380 --> 00:30:46.920
or how important they are,
but we know they're out there.

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:50.160
And then finally, the
biggest legacy we have

00:30:50.160 --> 00:30:55.050
of the 20th century is the
matter of crossing borders.

00:30:55.050 --> 00:30:57.270
Just about everybody
on this planet

00:30:57.270 --> 00:31:00.640
is on the move for
one reason or another.

00:31:00.640 --> 00:31:07.260
And every move is related to
some increment of energy use.

00:31:07.260 --> 00:31:10.020
The statistics that
we have about mobility

00:31:10.020 --> 00:31:16.080
of people and the venues and
the duration and the location

00:31:16.080 --> 00:31:18.750
and the sources and
the consequences

00:31:18.750 --> 00:31:23.490
are not quite synchronized
with the knowledge

00:31:23.490 --> 00:31:28.170
that we need with respect to
the associated energy uses.

00:31:28.170 --> 00:31:34.240
So first, the familiar which
is more people on this planet

00:31:34.240 --> 00:31:37.380
that I don't need to say
anything more about it.

00:31:37.380 --> 00:31:41.430
The insert here is perhaps
a little less familiar,

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:44.460
and this is, believe
it or not, there

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:50.190
has been a slight improvement
in the human condition

00:31:50.190 --> 00:31:52.890
since over the past
century or so in the sense

00:31:52.890 --> 00:31:56.890
that the share of the
poor has declined.

00:31:56.890 --> 00:31:59.370
Now you may consider
that good news,

00:31:59.370 --> 00:32:02.010
but placed in the
context of the demands

00:32:02.010 --> 00:32:06.930
and the needs of the poor
population, that's not

00:32:06.930 --> 00:32:08.890
as good news as we would want.

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:12.330
And then let's factor in
the energy requirements

00:32:12.330 --> 00:32:17.310
almost by necessity, as we
all become wealthier, richer,

00:32:17.310 --> 00:32:19.570
better off, and so forth.

00:32:19.570 --> 00:32:23.010
And here's a minor footnote--

00:32:23.010 --> 00:32:27.030
the individual-- Joe Smith,
let's say, John Doe--

00:32:27.030 --> 00:32:33.330
living in China is embedded
in an energy infrastructure

00:32:33.330 --> 00:32:34.770
that is quite different.

00:32:34.770 --> 00:32:38.760
So his own individualized
per capita consumption

00:32:38.760 --> 00:32:41.910
is very different than if Joe
Smith or John Doe, whoever

00:32:41.910 --> 00:32:44.250
it is moves to Los Angeles.

00:32:44.250 --> 00:32:47.560
And the same individual, nothing
is strange about him or her--

00:32:47.560 --> 00:32:50.910
simply the infrastructure within
which he works has changed.

00:32:50.910 --> 00:32:56.500
And there the energy numbers
look quite different.

00:32:56.500 --> 00:33:00.100
As a simple reminder as to
the mobility of people--

00:33:00.100 --> 00:33:03.400
now this is very misleading
because I should give you

00:33:03.400 --> 00:33:05.740
the other side of it, which
I'm not going to give you--

00:33:05.740 --> 00:33:10.330
and that is the
percentage of the migrants

00:33:10.330 --> 00:33:14.740
to the total population,
which is not in this case.

00:33:14.740 --> 00:33:17.050
The only thing I want to
draw your attention to

00:33:17.050 --> 00:33:21.190
is that movement across
borders generating

00:33:21.190 --> 00:33:25.960
a stock measured at one point
in time is very, very large.

00:33:25.960 --> 00:33:30.770
And the word migrant here
refers to a voluntary movement--

00:33:30.770 --> 00:33:34.760
people who willingly
decide to cross borders.

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:37.300
Most people, let me
put it differently.

00:33:37.300 --> 00:33:40.600
That's a large
fraction of crossing

00:33:40.600 --> 00:33:45.970
borders that is not voluntary.

00:33:45.970 --> 00:33:52.620
That too carries with it some
energy-related coefficients,

00:33:52.620 --> 00:33:55.350
leaving aside the coefficients
related to justice,

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:59.040
quality of life, et cetera.

00:33:59.040 --> 00:34:05.190
So what I've covered so far
are related to basic facts.

00:34:05.190 --> 00:34:07.660
Let's talk about some surprises.

00:34:07.660 --> 00:34:14.739
And this is where I ask you
to consider the implications.

00:34:14.739 --> 00:34:18.480
The first surprise has to do
with the unexpected, declines

00:34:18.480 --> 00:34:19.679
in fertility.

00:34:19.679 --> 00:34:23.110
We had never thought 50
years ago, 60 years ago,

00:34:23.110 --> 00:34:24.330
30 years ago--

00:34:24.330 --> 00:34:28.320
over 30 years ago when I joined
the MIT faculty that we would

00:34:28.320 --> 00:34:30.359
consider this an issue decline.

00:34:33.070 --> 00:34:35.679
And I'll get to do that
in more that in a minute.

00:34:35.679 --> 00:34:40.420
The second surprise is the
dependency ratio in population.

00:34:40.420 --> 00:34:44.100
And then finally,
the aging society--

00:34:44.100 --> 00:34:46.639
the aging society
is a correlate.

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.989
It is connected
to, tightly coupled

00:34:48.989 --> 00:34:53.040
tightly with the
industrializing society.

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:55.739
So the question
to you is the quiz

00:34:55.739 --> 00:35:00.210
for this morning is, do you
see any energy-related impacts

00:35:00.210 --> 00:35:03.000
either on the demand side
or on the distribution

00:35:03.000 --> 00:35:05.640
side or other infrastructure
requirements side,

00:35:05.640 --> 00:35:08.550
or is this just a blip
in our statistics?

00:35:08.550 --> 00:35:11.280
And hey, life is complicated.

00:35:11.280 --> 00:35:17.760
So let's look at the
fertility declines.

00:35:17.760 --> 00:35:24.390
What you see here is a fairly
good representation of what

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:27.150
we now understand
to be a basic fact,

00:35:27.150 --> 00:35:30.730
and this is in
industrialized countries,

00:35:30.730 --> 00:35:35.010
the populations are not
reproducing themselves.

00:35:35.010 --> 00:35:38.880
Now we consider that a problem.

00:35:38.880 --> 00:35:41.160
One could consider
that a problem.

00:35:41.160 --> 00:35:43.710
In the old days, the only
problem we're familiar with

00:35:43.710 --> 00:35:45.060
is rapid population growth.

00:35:45.060 --> 00:35:48.930
Too many people are
having too many children.

00:35:48.930 --> 00:35:51.960
And I'm not passing
judgment here.

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:54.180
I'm simply saying that
this is a surprise

00:35:54.180 --> 00:35:55.840
because we had not expected it.

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:57.180
We had not looked for it.

00:35:57.180 --> 00:35:59.250
We had not anticipated it.

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:03.210
And this comes from
the economists.

00:36:03.210 --> 00:36:07.410
And I just love this one
for the following reasons.

00:36:07.410 --> 00:36:11.550
There were plenty of clues
before the popular press

00:36:11.550 --> 00:36:15.240
picked up the fact that the
Russians were disappearing.

00:36:15.240 --> 00:36:17.880
And here it has to do
mainly with the fertility

00:36:17.880 --> 00:36:23.340
side that the death rate and
the life expectancy of Russian--

00:36:23.340 --> 00:36:26.220
then the Soviet
population-- was going down,

00:36:26.220 --> 00:36:28.740
having to do with
health-related issues,

00:36:28.740 --> 00:36:31.950
having to do with inhaling
too many stuff that's

00:36:31.950 --> 00:36:35.910
not good for you, et cetera.

00:36:35.910 --> 00:36:40.440
And we saw that long
before were able to connect

00:36:40.440 --> 00:36:44.190
energy, climate change
potentials, pollution,

00:36:44.190 --> 00:36:45.450
and health--

00:36:45.450 --> 00:36:50.010
pieces that we now take for
granted as going together.

00:36:50.010 --> 00:36:52.920
This is not very good news
for the Russians-- at least

00:36:52.920 --> 00:36:56.280
I don't think it's
good news for them.

00:36:56.280 --> 00:36:58.320
And the standard
policy that countries

00:36:58.320 --> 00:37:02.550
have when faced with news
that they don't consider good

00:37:02.550 --> 00:37:04.860
is to try to do something.

00:37:04.860 --> 00:37:08.040
And we know based on the history
of the advanced industrial

00:37:08.040 --> 00:37:10.950
countries that it
is any policy to try

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:12.870
to increase the fertility rate.

00:37:12.870 --> 00:37:14.850
And an advanced
industrial country

00:37:14.850 --> 00:37:18.390
is doomed to failure--
has failed to no end.

00:37:18.390 --> 00:37:22.415
Case in point here
would be France.

00:37:22.415 --> 00:37:24.540
This is probably more than
you really want to know,

00:37:24.540 --> 00:37:26.760
but it's not connecting
much more closely

00:37:26.760 --> 00:37:28.800
to the energy issue.

00:37:28.800 --> 00:37:33.270
And I just want to
draw your attention

00:37:33.270 --> 00:37:39.550
to the solid black line, which
is the rising dependency ratio,

00:37:39.550 --> 00:37:45.210
which is the ratio of the
non-working population

00:37:45.210 --> 00:37:47.330
to the working population.

00:37:47.330 --> 00:37:50.670
And we really don't want to
have this dependency ratio go up

00:37:50.670 --> 00:37:53.430
because it means that the
working population has

00:37:53.430 --> 00:37:57.552
to support a larger fraction
of the total population.

00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:03.030
So on the quiz sheet
for this morning

00:38:03.030 --> 00:38:07.770
is, is this trend that we
now think is fairly robust--

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:13.130
is it neutral with respect to
energy effects on any dimension

00:38:13.130 --> 00:38:14.670
of the energy issue?

00:38:18.500 --> 00:38:21.770
Now we move on to
the technology parts.

00:38:21.770 --> 00:38:24.980
And in this audience,
I will not presume

00:38:24.980 --> 00:38:31.100
to say anything technical
on the technology aspect.

00:38:31.100 --> 00:38:35.540
But I will focus specifically
on three issues that

00:38:35.540 --> 00:38:40.070
are fundamentally important
legacies of the 20th century

00:38:40.070 --> 00:38:43.250
and are going to face
shape how we deal

00:38:43.250 --> 00:38:46.400
with the 21st century
on energy issues

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:49.320
and just about everything
else under the sun.

00:38:49.320 --> 00:38:52.550
The first is globalization and
the persistence of this thing

00:38:52.550 --> 00:38:55.190
that we call globalization.

00:38:55.190 --> 00:38:58.070
We did the rough census of
what globalization means,

00:38:58.070 --> 00:39:01.250
and I won't bore you with
varieties of definitions

00:39:01.250 --> 00:39:05.780
that we came up with
in the literature

00:39:05.780 --> 00:39:07.320
and in the popular press.

00:39:07.320 --> 00:39:10.910
But the fact is everything
is crossing borders,

00:39:10.910 --> 00:39:16.770
whether it's people, ideas,
things, and so forth.

00:39:16.770 --> 00:39:19.400
The second issue
is globalization.

00:39:19.400 --> 00:39:21.830
And no, I did not
make this word up.

00:39:21.830 --> 00:39:26.420
This comes from the
United Nations' coining

00:39:26.420 --> 00:39:31.400
of this concept to reflect the
conjunction of globalization

00:39:31.400 --> 00:39:35.970
processes but the localization
effects of globalization--

00:39:35.970 --> 00:39:41.720
so the linkages between
globalization and localization.

00:39:41.720 --> 00:39:44.390
The interesting thing
here is that we now

00:39:44.390 --> 00:39:49.640
know that are many local
effects that have been generated

00:39:49.640 --> 00:39:52.560
by the globalization process.

00:39:52.560 --> 00:39:53.930
So it's not a decoupling.

00:39:53.930 --> 00:39:56.510
It's a recoupling
in a different way.

00:39:56.510 --> 00:40:01.830
What we don't yet know is
how we can connect this fact,

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:05.660
I would say, to our energy
policies, our energy

00:40:05.660 --> 00:40:09.650
strategies, and of course, to
the application of innovation

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:10.370
in this area.

00:40:10.370 --> 00:40:15.630
So consider this the
next item on the quiz.

00:40:15.630 --> 00:40:19.820
The third energy issue that
is very, very important

00:40:19.820 --> 00:40:24.290
goes without saying is the
global race for knowledge.

00:40:24.290 --> 00:40:27.480
It's not the global arms
race of the olden days.

00:40:27.480 --> 00:40:32.240
But it is a race, nonetheless.

00:40:32.240 --> 00:40:34.850
And what I will
draw your attention

00:40:34.850 --> 00:40:38.120
to in just in just a
minute is the impact

00:40:38.120 --> 00:40:45.230
of this race on one particular
domain, and that is the shifts.

00:40:45.230 --> 00:40:51.100
What has this done to shift
the way in which we organize

00:40:51.100 --> 00:40:55.220
and think about our
social activities

00:40:55.220 --> 00:40:58.940
and about where value added
is or added value is--

00:41:02.250 --> 00:41:04.760
sources of added value?

00:41:04.760 --> 00:41:09.000
There are multiple
indicators of globalization.

00:41:09.000 --> 00:41:11.910
I don't need to persuade you
that there is something going

00:41:11.910 --> 00:41:13.550
on out there in the world.

00:41:13.550 --> 00:41:16.540
At the simplest, a
trend I could get around

00:41:16.540 --> 00:41:18.540
which there would be no
controversy more or less

00:41:18.540 --> 00:41:19.470
is world trade.

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:22.080
So take that as an indicator.

00:41:22.080 --> 00:41:25.410
Most interesting,
however, is the impact

00:41:25.410 --> 00:41:27.695
of knowledge and density--

00:41:31.850 --> 00:41:36.300
the impact of shifting
this structure of economies

00:41:36.300 --> 00:41:39.010
and the structure of societies.

00:41:39.010 --> 00:41:41.050
And we're moving
in that direction.

00:41:41.050 --> 00:41:46.180
And as we go down that path,
what implications are there

00:41:46.180 --> 00:41:47.920
for energy?

00:41:47.920 --> 00:41:51.580
I would like to think that
it's got to be only good news,

00:41:51.580 --> 00:41:53.650
but I'm an optimist.

00:41:53.650 --> 00:41:55.650
So what we've already
started to see

00:41:55.650 --> 00:41:58.200
as a legacy from
the 20th century

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:02.520
is a gradual shift from the
strict thinking about supply

00:42:02.520 --> 00:42:05.580
chain requirements as
being anchors of meeting

00:42:05.580 --> 00:42:08.640
supply chain requirements
as being anchors

00:42:08.640 --> 00:42:11.670
due to the economy and to
the social order to thinking

00:42:11.670 --> 00:42:14.520
about the knowledge chain.

00:42:14.520 --> 00:42:17.550
And what we're
doing here at MIT is

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:20.610
beginning to
formalize and clarify

00:42:20.610 --> 00:42:23.760
what would be meant
by the knowledge chain

00:42:23.760 --> 00:42:28.800
and at which point societies
gain by reducing disconnects

00:42:28.800 --> 00:42:33.570
among various links
to that chain.

00:42:33.570 --> 00:42:36.990
We've also seen shifts
from material output that

00:42:36.990 --> 00:42:41.160
wish to have expanded
material output to the wish

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:46.380
to have better ways of
meeting social needs.

00:42:46.380 --> 00:42:49.650
We're seeing shifts from
knowledge access reducing

00:42:49.650 --> 00:42:54.450
barriers to access to
enhancing, facilitating

00:42:54.450 --> 00:42:58.170
venues for knowledge generation
and knowledge creation.

00:42:58.170 --> 00:43:00.660
So we're beginning to
think of knowledge not

00:43:00.660 --> 00:43:03.510
as the residual in the
proverbial textbook

00:43:03.510 --> 00:43:07.590
case of the production
function in the old days,

00:43:07.590 --> 00:43:12.090
but as really endogenizing
the entire process.

00:43:12.090 --> 00:43:15.300
And then the final shift that
is the most fabulous of all

00:43:15.300 --> 00:43:18.300
at this point in time-- and
it was alluded to earlier

00:43:18.300 --> 00:43:23.970
by President Hockfield--
is that the interest

00:43:23.970 --> 00:43:29.340
that we have in contributing
to the diffusion

00:43:29.340 --> 00:43:34.710
and the distribution
of knowledge.

00:43:34.710 --> 00:43:40.320
Now this sounds very much like
motherhood and an apple pie,

00:43:40.320 --> 00:43:42.930
and I certainly don't
mean to sound that way.

00:43:42.930 --> 00:43:49.140
What I do want to say is that if
we can understand and unbundle

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:55.110
the knowledge chain and if we
can locate the value of doing

00:43:55.110 --> 00:44:00.060
this with respect to our energy
dilemmas, challenges, problems,

00:44:00.060 --> 00:44:07.580
we might be one step ahead in
framing our response policies.

00:44:07.580 --> 00:44:10.290
Surprises-- what
are the surprises

00:44:10.290 --> 00:44:16.110
from a socioeconomic and
political point of view.

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:21.000
And to me, the most
obvious surprise

00:44:21.000 --> 00:44:23.670
of all, looking back
at the 20th century

00:44:23.670 --> 00:44:30.710
and if we were anticipating
technological changes

00:44:30.710 --> 00:44:33.140
would have been very difficult
then at the beginning

00:44:33.140 --> 00:44:36.110
of the 20th century to
have correctly anticipating

00:44:36.110 --> 00:44:40.280
the forging of virtual space--

00:44:40.280 --> 00:44:43.310
cyberspace, the
internet, et cetera.

00:44:43.310 --> 00:44:48.440
To the architects of
this space, the focus

00:44:48.440 --> 00:44:50.660
has been on the
internet and the wires

00:44:50.660 --> 00:44:53.120
and whatever else
goes into this thing.

00:44:53.120 --> 00:44:56.420
But for the social
scientists and for the users

00:44:56.420 --> 00:44:59.070
is the creation
forging of new spaces,

00:44:59.070 --> 00:45:03.860
new arenas of interactions
and new voicing

00:45:03.860 --> 00:45:08.620
and new opportunities and
again, connect new voicing

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:11.360
and new opportunities to
who is doing the voicing

00:45:11.360 --> 00:45:13.550
and who is doing the
opportunities and what

00:45:13.550 --> 00:45:15.530
difference it might make.

00:45:15.530 --> 00:45:19.490
The last piece-- the
last mile, if you wish--

00:45:19.490 --> 00:45:23.000
is the wireless
connections as part

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:25.640
of this bundle of surprises.

00:45:25.640 --> 00:45:27.410
I'll get to that in
just a moment too.

00:45:27.410 --> 00:45:29.540
The second surprise
is that environment

00:45:29.540 --> 00:45:31.940
does, in fact, matter.

00:45:31.940 --> 00:45:36.120
It's not something that we
would have anticipated early

00:45:36.120 --> 00:45:39.320
in the 20th century.

00:45:39.320 --> 00:45:44.480
And then the third
surprise is the dark side--

00:45:44.480 --> 00:45:48.290
that states are at
risk from factors

00:45:48.290 --> 00:45:52.640
that we did not expect
them to be at risk from.

00:45:52.640 --> 00:45:57.160
So what now who or what next?

00:45:57.160 --> 00:46:02.530
Opportunities-- this is
an example of internet

00:46:02.530 --> 00:46:03.543
generated revenues.

00:46:03.543 --> 00:46:05.710
I don't have the sources
here for which I apologize,

00:46:05.710 --> 00:46:11.650
but it comes from
an economist report.

00:46:11.650 --> 00:46:17.450
And the amount in the axis is
in the Bs, of course, so more.

00:46:17.450 --> 00:46:21.110
With more comes more
regulatory stuff.

00:46:21.110 --> 00:46:23.690
As a political scientist,
I take it for granted

00:46:23.690 --> 00:46:30.180
that any new activity creates
a new bureaucracy of some sort.

00:46:30.180 --> 00:46:34.230
Now that is not
really good news.

00:46:34.230 --> 00:46:37.040
It may be necessary, but
it's not really good news

00:46:37.040 --> 00:46:42.230
because we know that major,
major, major disconnects

00:46:42.230 --> 00:46:46.190
in just basic communication
among bureaucracies

00:46:46.190 --> 00:46:53.990
and among agencies who have the
same mission statement create.

00:46:53.990 --> 00:46:55.760
We're creating a
headache for ourselves

00:46:55.760 --> 00:46:58.670
that we may not
necessarily want to.

00:46:58.670 --> 00:47:03.680
Another part of the
technological surprise-- and I

00:47:03.680 --> 00:47:07.790
put it on the side
of technology here

00:47:07.790 --> 00:47:11.000
as opposed to on the side of the
ledger of the social scientists

00:47:11.000 --> 00:47:14.240
who could have tried a little
harder to anticipate some

00:47:14.240 --> 00:47:18.530
of those differences-- there
are two pieces of the surprises

00:47:18.530 --> 00:47:21.000
that are important in terms
of the environmental effects

00:47:21.000 --> 00:47:24.320
of our industrialization
trajectory.

00:47:24.320 --> 00:47:28.820
One is the differential impacts
across and within countries,

00:47:28.820 --> 00:47:30.050
which you know.

00:47:30.050 --> 00:47:33.950
But second is that there
are going to be already

00:47:33.950 --> 00:47:37.130
but will continue
to be more burdens

00:47:37.130 --> 00:47:40.500
on the poor everywhere
in all societies,

00:47:40.500 --> 00:47:42.650
this society as well.

00:47:42.650 --> 00:47:46.220
Does energy policy
factor into this at all?

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:49.340
By now, you may think that
the connection between energy

00:47:49.340 --> 00:47:55.170
and the issues that I'm
raising is far too long.

00:47:55.170 --> 00:47:57.200
The chain there is too long.

00:47:57.200 --> 00:47:59.670
But let me assure
that it really is not

00:47:59.670 --> 00:48:04.850
if you consider it
carefully because we're also

00:48:04.850 --> 00:48:07.520
seeing, anticipating
the prospects of more

00:48:07.520 --> 00:48:12.530
social cleavages as potential
effects of climate change

00:48:12.530 --> 00:48:14.660
arise.

00:48:14.660 --> 00:48:22.510
The next item on this
checklist is the erosion

00:48:22.510 --> 00:48:25.750
of the power of governance
and the power of institutions

00:48:25.750 --> 00:48:33.590
if they get overloaded by the
effects of the transformations

00:48:33.590 --> 00:48:37.170
in the natural environment.

00:48:37.170 --> 00:48:43.020
Without spending too
much time on this,

00:48:43.020 --> 00:48:46.530
the other side of the
loss of law and order

00:48:46.530 --> 00:48:50.550
is the growth in individual
entrepreneurship-- self-help,

00:48:50.550 --> 00:48:51.480
if you wish.

00:48:51.480 --> 00:48:53.460
And remember societies
are organized

00:48:53.460 --> 00:48:57.150
to prevent excessive self-help.

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:03.180
So what we worry about
here is that we have not

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:06.780
thought enough about the
potential consequences

00:49:06.780 --> 00:49:11.430
on the social order of
this particular surprise.

00:49:11.430 --> 00:49:15.350
And now we understand that
this is terrifically important

00:49:15.350 --> 00:49:17.910
and where are-- the at least
in the [INAUDIBLE] science

00:49:17.910 --> 00:49:20.190
department--

00:49:20.190 --> 00:49:24.840
spending serious time
looking at that issue.

00:49:28.170 --> 00:49:33.940
There is some attempt to measure
the international impacts

00:49:33.940 --> 00:49:36.140
of potential climate change.

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:38.170
If you notice a highlight
potential just in case

00:49:38.170 --> 00:49:41.070
the controversy
is on this issue.

00:49:41.070 --> 00:49:47.530
And the climate change
index is now considered

00:49:47.530 --> 00:49:52.000
as plausibly accurate with
respect to countries at risk--

00:49:52.000 --> 00:49:55.480
at risk with respect
to health hazards,

00:49:55.480 --> 00:49:57.520
physical, and
physical conditions

00:49:57.520 --> 00:50:00.460
as well as immediate
environmental impact

00:50:00.460 --> 00:50:01.720
or environmental dislocation.

00:50:04.290 --> 00:50:05.960
This is not really
very good news.

00:50:05.960 --> 00:50:09.850
But I think that as we think
about the energy, climate

00:50:09.850 --> 00:50:12.460
or energy environment
linkages, we're

00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:17.010
going to have to
recognize that there

00:50:17.010 --> 00:50:21.240
are significant high
probability outcomes

00:50:21.240 --> 00:50:27.080
that we're going to have to
deal with somewhat seriously.

00:50:27.080 --> 00:50:32.550
And I identified sharing
with you two of those here.

00:50:32.550 --> 00:50:35.940
The first bullet is
respective 10 countries

00:50:35.940 --> 00:50:39.420
that are high in
terms of overall risk,

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:41.400
and you have the list
of the countries here.

00:50:41.400 --> 00:50:44.610
But these are
countries that so far

00:50:44.610 --> 00:50:49.040
account for a very small amount
of greenhouse gas emissions.

00:50:49.040 --> 00:50:50.970
Some of these
countries will be going

00:50:50.970 --> 00:50:54.610
under reconstruction programs.

00:50:54.610 --> 00:50:58.800
And so the challenge
is how can we

00:50:58.800 --> 00:51:03.460
best engage in reconstruction
without putting in place

00:51:03.460 --> 00:51:06.330
the energy economy
interactions that

00:51:06.330 --> 00:51:09.360
were destroyed to begin with?

00:51:09.360 --> 00:51:13.800
And President Hockfield
referred to efficiencies

00:51:13.800 --> 00:51:15.810
on the MIT campus.

00:51:15.810 --> 00:51:20.530
Can efficiencies
be imposed upon,

00:51:20.530 --> 00:51:25.510
transferred portable to
large-scale reconstruction

00:51:25.510 --> 00:51:27.230
efforts.

00:51:27.230 --> 00:51:30.580
We hope so, but
the records so far

00:51:30.580 --> 00:51:34.420
in terms of the reconstruction
of destroyed economies

00:51:34.420 --> 00:51:38.920
over the last decade
or so shows us

00:51:38.920 --> 00:51:41.890
that we are very good
at reconstructing

00:51:41.890 --> 00:51:43.510
what we've already destroyed.

00:51:43.510 --> 00:51:46.330
We're not good at
putting in place

00:51:46.330 --> 00:51:48.880
the new generations of
technologies that are already

00:51:48.880 --> 00:51:51.490
available to us.

00:51:51.490 --> 00:51:55.690
Okay, this is a simple
reminder of the distribution

00:51:55.690 --> 00:51:58.720
of who's going to be
in pain on this one

00:51:58.720 --> 00:52:07.310
and without any statement
about the nature of the pain.

00:52:07.310 --> 00:52:10.020
And now I get to
the darker side--

00:52:10.020 --> 00:52:12.230
the countries at risk--

00:52:12.230 --> 00:52:16.130
at risk of conflict
or at risk of being

00:52:16.130 --> 00:52:19.190
the recipient of
unhappiness that

00:52:19.190 --> 00:52:22.370
is generated
elsewhere associated

00:52:22.370 --> 00:52:26.180
with climate-related issues.

00:52:26.180 --> 00:52:28.360
There is a concentration,
as you can see,

00:52:28.360 --> 00:52:30.780
in the African continent.

00:52:30.780 --> 00:52:33.920
There are also a bunch
of other concentrations.

00:52:33.920 --> 00:52:38.810
The interesting thing about this
is that this assessment comes

00:52:38.810 --> 00:52:44.400
out of the community
that focuses

00:52:44.400 --> 00:52:48.300
on climate-related
impacts, and the fact

00:52:48.300 --> 00:52:52.800
that they're concerned with
conflict at exactly the time

00:52:52.800 --> 00:52:56.130
in which the political conflict
community in political science

00:52:56.130 --> 00:52:59.070
is beginning to think about
the environmental effect

00:52:59.070 --> 00:53:02.070
is an example of
the type of coupling

00:53:02.070 --> 00:53:04.620
that we've started to
do and we're getting

00:53:04.620 --> 00:53:07.870
to be much more involved with.

00:53:07.870 --> 00:53:11.310
And let me stress
the obvious by way

00:53:11.310 --> 00:53:14.900
of indicating how important
this is, the coupling part,

00:53:14.900 --> 00:53:18.330
and that is the social
sciences as all of the sciences

00:53:18.330 --> 00:53:22.020
but only with the social
sciences were framed

00:53:22.020 --> 00:53:26.160
and developed over time
by very carefully carving

00:53:26.160 --> 00:53:31.770
out, partitioning human activity
out of the natural environment

00:53:31.770 --> 00:53:34.900
and looking at the space and
then very carefully cutting out

00:53:34.900 --> 00:53:36.520
pieces of human activity.

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:40.390
So you have the politics for the
political scientists, economics

00:53:40.390 --> 00:53:46.050
for the economists, and so forth
knowing that the world is not

00:53:46.050 --> 00:53:49.410
that way, but understanding
that this is how we

00:53:49.410 --> 00:53:51.840
will generate knowledge faster.

00:53:51.840 --> 00:53:54.000
Now we realize that
the strategy that

00:53:54.000 --> 00:53:56.430
has been very successful
throughout the 20th century

00:53:56.430 --> 00:53:59.790
and before is a major
liability today.

00:53:59.790 --> 00:54:01.800
So now what we're
trying to do is

00:54:01.800 --> 00:54:06.000
put Humpty Dumpty together
again but in a way that does not

00:54:06.000 --> 00:54:09.390
undermine the growth
and the strength

00:54:09.390 --> 00:54:13.170
of the individual disciplines
and in the individual fields.

00:54:13.170 --> 00:54:16.590
Needless to say, this is
easier said than done.

00:54:16.590 --> 00:54:20.220
But the more important and
the more serious part of this

00:54:20.220 --> 00:54:25.950
is that the need
to reconnect is one

00:54:25.950 --> 00:54:29.880
that comes from within
the fields, so there's

00:54:29.880 --> 00:54:31.710
a very powerful
realization that we

00:54:31.710 --> 00:54:34.920
have to reshape our system
boundaries and our field

00:54:34.920 --> 00:54:37.240
boundaries.

00:54:37.240 --> 00:54:51.130
Now the issue of energy basics,
the third set of legacies.

00:54:51.130 --> 00:54:53.650
Fossil fuels, the usual--

00:54:53.650 --> 00:54:56.840
I don't need to
review that with you.

00:54:56.840 --> 00:55:01.870
The second legacy has
to do with the continued

00:55:01.870 --> 00:55:04.300
politicization of energy.

00:55:04.300 --> 00:55:08.050
Energy, as you know, is
intensely political issues

00:55:08.050 --> 00:55:12.100
across the globe--

00:55:12.100 --> 00:55:15.490
maybe with the exception
of legislation budgets.

00:55:15.490 --> 00:55:17.770
There's a search
for alternatives--

00:55:17.770 --> 00:55:20.690
the usual, maybe
more than usual.

00:55:20.690 --> 00:55:23.110
But then we have the mess
in the Middle East, which

00:55:23.110 --> 00:55:26.230
is clearly more than the usual.

00:55:26.230 --> 00:55:28.300
When you put all
these things together,

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:30.880
we have some immediate
short-term dilemmas.

00:55:30.880 --> 00:55:32.380
We won't call them crises.

00:55:32.380 --> 00:55:41.420
And Professor Deutsch may
address those in a few minutes.

00:55:44.260 --> 00:55:49.280
But what I'd like to
do is share with you

00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:53.390
the view that the usual
is a bit more messy

00:55:53.390 --> 00:55:56.250
than it has been in the past.

00:55:56.250 --> 00:56:01.040
This is a simple reminder
of the flow of exports

00:56:01.040 --> 00:56:05.090
of oil and global oil.

00:56:05.090 --> 00:56:08.030
I just picked this one
up because the message

00:56:08.030 --> 00:56:11.960
is very, very clear.

00:56:11.960 --> 00:56:16.160
And you see the salience and the
centrality of the Middle East

00:56:16.160 --> 00:56:24.300
and followed by a vision from
The Boston Globe in 1986.

00:56:24.300 --> 00:56:28.330
This actually is very accurate.

00:56:28.330 --> 00:56:31.080
'86 is decades ago.

00:56:35.270 --> 00:56:37.220
I mean, you're
familiar with this,

00:56:37.220 --> 00:56:40.340
but there is, unfortunately,
one more angle on that,

00:56:40.340 --> 00:56:42.380
which is this way.

00:56:42.380 --> 00:56:45.920
The message here may not
be what you think it is.

00:56:45.920 --> 00:56:52.610
It has to do with in addition
to this mess, we have a mess

00:56:52.610 --> 00:56:56.450
where the many, many players--

00:56:56.450 --> 00:57:01.340
not all of which are controlled
by states, and not all of which

00:57:01.340 --> 00:57:05.160
define winning and
losing the same way.

00:57:05.160 --> 00:57:08.540
This is the complexity
of the Middle East.

00:57:08.540 --> 00:57:12.950
The good news is that this
particular cartoon is not

00:57:12.950 --> 00:57:17.570
about a major oil-producing or
natural gas producing region.

00:57:17.570 --> 00:57:24.710
It is differences of perspective
over a non-oil-producing area.

00:57:24.710 --> 00:57:27.260
So if we take this
cartoon and put it

00:57:27.260 --> 00:57:31.250
on the future of Saudi
Arabia or the future of Iran

00:57:31.250 --> 00:57:36.830
or the future of you name
your favorite oil producer,

00:57:36.830 --> 00:57:39.550
we have a slightly
complicated situation

00:57:39.550 --> 00:57:46.280
that's going to create
imperatives for us right now.

00:57:46.280 --> 00:57:51.655
This is one of the most powerful
legacies of the 20th century.

00:57:54.460 --> 00:57:58.450
Just to show you that I'm
able to be even-handed on this

00:57:58.450 --> 00:58:00.270
comes from Time Magazine.

00:58:00.270 --> 00:58:02.090
You may not be able to see it.

00:58:02.090 --> 00:58:05.720
So I'll tell you why
this is interesting.

00:58:05.720 --> 00:58:09.490
It is a set of instances
in which the good guys--

00:58:09.490 --> 00:58:13.810
by the good guys, I mean
countries like Norway--

00:58:13.810 --> 00:58:17.480
begin to do some rather
unpleasant things

00:58:17.480 --> 00:58:21.230
over resource-related matters
and energy-related matters.

00:58:21.230 --> 00:58:27.160
And the case in
which the clashes--

00:58:27.160 --> 00:58:31.180
this is a case of Japan
between Japanese fishermen

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:33.820
and the Russian Navy.

00:58:33.820 --> 00:58:39.280
And then we have the Chinese
and [INAUDIBLE] this right--

00:58:43.630 --> 00:58:48.780
oh, sorry, shouldn't
touch this stuff--

00:58:48.780 --> 00:58:49.280
help.

00:58:57.264 --> 00:58:58.262
Can somebody come?

00:59:02.254 --> 00:59:04.749
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:04.749 --> 00:59:07.073
I'm sorry.

00:59:07.073 --> 00:59:08.990
I'm not going to be
embarrassed, so don't try.

00:59:11.910 --> 00:59:18.870
Okay, eventually, you can
get the hard copy of this.

00:59:18.870 --> 00:59:21.090
I apologize because
it's not very clear.

00:59:21.090 --> 00:59:26.130
But the Russians tried
to pressure the Georgians

00:59:26.130 --> 00:59:30.210
by boycotting their wine
and their mineral water

00:59:30.210 --> 00:59:38.380
over some dispute, and
that had some connections

00:59:38.380 --> 00:59:42.610
to the pricing of imports
and exports of commodities.

00:59:42.610 --> 00:59:45.730
Now the surprise in the
energy from the social science

00:59:45.730 --> 00:59:47.950
point of view.

00:59:47.950 --> 00:59:51.130
First, we hadn't expected the
breakdown of the Soviet Union.

00:59:51.130 --> 00:59:53.890
It came too quick, too
fast, and too thorough.

00:59:53.890 --> 00:59:57.970
But what it did do
is shift control

00:59:57.970 --> 01:00:01.330
over known energy resources.

01:00:01.330 --> 01:00:05.225
The new state owners
have new priorities.

01:00:05.225 --> 01:00:06.850
And most of the time,
we're not exactly

01:00:06.850 --> 01:00:09.790
sure what their priorities are,
so the uncertainties for us

01:00:09.790 --> 01:00:10.630
go up.

01:00:10.630 --> 01:00:13.283
And I do want to talk
about security issues.

01:00:13.283 --> 01:00:14.200
This is just legacies.

01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:22.550
And the bottom line of this on
the political economy of energy

01:00:22.550 --> 01:00:25.610
that's been handed to
us from the 20th century

01:00:25.610 --> 01:00:29.650
is uncertainties about
who controls what

01:00:29.650 --> 01:00:33.700
in the shorter time horizon.

01:00:33.700 --> 01:00:37.580
Now to the fourth and
last part of the legacy

01:00:37.580 --> 01:00:43.810
of the 20th century.

01:00:43.810 --> 01:00:45.640
I've picked up what
I believe to be

01:00:45.640 --> 01:00:48.160
the ones that will
remain powerful

01:00:48.160 --> 01:00:51.700
throughout the 21st century.

01:00:51.700 --> 01:00:55.240
The salience of environment
is very, very, very unlikely

01:00:55.240 --> 01:00:58.960
that this is an issue
that will go away

01:00:58.960 --> 01:01:02.620
unless we become unbelievably
creative more than we

01:01:02.620 --> 01:01:04.480
can imagine.

01:01:04.480 --> 01:01:07.163
The evidence that we have
so far-- and regretfully,

01:01:07.163 --> 01:01:08.830
I don't have that on
the slide for you--

01:01:08.830 --> 01:01:13.750
is the enormous growth of
international environmental

01:01:13.750 --> 01:01:14.650
treaties--

01:01:14.650 --> 01:01:16.720
multilateral treaties.

01:01:16.720 --> 01:01:19.210
This is not treaties
that contain

01:01:19.210 --> 01:01:22.150
an environmental
component clause in it,

01:01:22.150 --> 01:01:25.360
but focusing specifically
on environment.

01:01:25.360 --> 01:01:27.460
And the most interesting
thing about that

01:01:27.460 --> 01:01:33.400
is that the trend of global
environmental treaty-making

01:01:33.400 --> 01:01:37.720
looks exactly the same as
the trend of US participation

01:01:37.720 --> 01:01:39.110
environmental treaty-making.

01:01:39.110 --> 01:01:41.470
So all things
considered, what we've

01:01:41.470 --> 01:01:45.820
gotten from the 20th century is
a very high participation rate

01:01:45.820 --> 01:01:47.830
by the United States.

01:01:47.830 --> 01:01:51.070
The second major issue
on the global agenda

01:01:51.070 --> 01:01:54.520
that is clearly
new is the issue of

01:01:54.520 --> 01:01:57.560
sustainability and
sustainable development.

01:01:57.560 --> 01:02:00.670
And now when you
drill into the issue,

01:02:00.670 --> 01:02:03.760
we come up against
sustainability, sustainable

01:02:03.760 --> 01:02:09.430
energy as strategies for the
sustainableness of energy

01:02:09.430 --> 01:02:10.120
issues.

01:02:10.120 --> 01:02:13.240
That, of course, is part of
the research agenda here.

01:02:15.970 --> 01:02:20.230
The next item that is part
of the global agenda--

01:02:20.230 --> 01:02:24.670
and by global agenda, I mean the
global political agenda and its

01:02:24.670 --> 01:02:28.240
connections to our
own research agenda--

01:02:28.240 --> 01:02:31.840
is getting a better
handle on the not

01:02:31.840 --> 01:02:36.130
so much the sources of
potentials for climate change.

01:02:36.130 --> 01:02:38.530
This is fairly well understood.

01:02:38.530 --> 01:02:43.570
What is not understood is the
nature of the consequences

01:02:43.570 --> 01:02:46.210
once you get beyond
the general averages

01:02:46.210 --> 01:02:50.410
or the general statements,
particularly those consequences

01:02:50.410 --> 01:02:55.510
for which it is possible to
intervene today effectively

01:02:55.510 --> 01:02:59.230
piece of an important
research agenda.

01:02:59.230 --> 01:03:04.120
The fourth item on the new
global agenda for politics

01:03:04.120 --> 01:03:08.410
and the world and
for the academy

01:03:08.410 --> 01:03:10.330
is revisiting the fundamentals.

01:03:10.330 --> 01:03:12.700
The fundamentals and
economic performance

01:03:12.700 --> 01:03:17.000
and economic growth in the past
has always been efficiency.

01:03:17.000 --> 01:03:19.240
Efficiency is really important.

01:03:19.240 --> 01:03:25.210
Now we factor in looking for
the possibility of coupling

01:03:25.210 --> 01:03:27.970
efficiency and equity.

01:03:27.970 --> 01:03:30.690
Can it be done?

01:03:30.690 --> 01:03:33.580
We are assuming
that it can be done.

01:03:33.580 --> 01:03:37.080
So the pursuit of efficiency
in economic activity,

01:03:37.080 --> 01:03:41.280
in policy but constraining
that in some fashion

01:03:41.280 --> 01:03:46.620
with a serious
attention to equity--

01:03:46.620 --> 01:03:48.280
equity measures of equity.

01:03:48.280 --> 01:03:53.580
And this then leads
us to the final item

01:03:53.580 --> 01:03:56.820
on the new global agenda that
I would like to share with you,

01:03:56.820 --> 01:04:01.050
and that is there is a trend out
there in the political world,

01:04:01.050 --> 01:04:08.930
in the UN world seeking to
define global public goods.

01:04:08.930 --> 01:04:15.970
And at academy, were trying to
articulate how one can actually

01:04:15.970 --> 01:04:20.410
think about funding money,
articulating funding

01:04:20.410 --> 01:04:26.270
and implementing any notion
of global public goods.

01:04:26.270 --> 01:04:28.510
This is more than international.

01:04:28.510 --> 01:04:30.040
This is more than transnational.

01:04:30.040 --> 01:04:32.860
This is addressing the
nature and the composition

01:04:32.860 --> 01:04:37.060
of the responsibility that civil
society at the global level

01:04:37.060 --> 01:04:40.090
has to itself.

01:04:40.090 --> 01:04:46.470
So the dangers on the
environment side--

01:04:46.470 --> 01:04:49.090
we've gone through this before.

01:04:49.090 --> 01:04:52.140
But I do want to draw
your attention to the very

01:04:52.140 --> 01:04:56.340
last item on that, and that is
matters related infrastructure.

01:04:56.340 --> 01:04:58.440
It goes without
saying that most of us

01:04:58.440 --> 01:05:02.730
believe that if there is serious
potential of climate change,

01:05:02.730 --> 01:05:06.700
if there is sea level
rise, et cetera, et cetera,

01:05:06.700 --> 01:05:09.540
the odds are very, very
high that our physical

01:05:09.540 --> 01:05:13.110
infrastructures are going to
be affected very, very deeply

01:05:13.110 --> 01:05:14.040
seriously.

01:05:14.040 --> 01:05:14.850
So what do we do?

01:05:18.260 --> 01:05:21.260
Balance sheet now on the
legacies of the 20th century--

01:05:21.260 --> 01:05:23.910
the good news and the bad news.

01:05:23.910 --> 01:05:26.750
The good news that we are going
to be calling the three Es

01:05:26.750 --> 01:05:28.670
is coupled with some bad
news that we're going

01:05:28.670 --> 01:05:31.070
to call the missing piece.

01:05:31.070 --> 01:05:36.080
The three Es is we
understand some,

01:05:36.080 --> 01:05:39.830
but we understand the need
for better understanding

01:05:39.830 --> 01:05:41.900
the interconnections
between energy, environment,

01:05:41.900 --> 01:05:42.470
and economy.

01:05:42.470 --> 01:05:45.500
That's a message
that we all have,

01:05:45.500 --> 01:05:49.520
and it's reflected in our
course offerings as well.

01:05:49.520 --> 01:05:50.960
That's the good news.

01:05:50.960 --> 01:05:54.290
Now the missing
piece or the bad news

01:05:54.290 --> 01:05:58.820
is that what we are ignoring
some important political

01:05:58.820 --> 01:06:01.370
realities because politics
is uncomfortable business,

01:06:01.370 --> 01:06:04.710
and we don't like to deal
with this unless we have to

01:06:04.710 --> 01:06:06.800
for professional reasons.

01:06:06.800 --> 01:06:10.230
And that is the reality
is our participation.

01:06:10.230 --> 01:06:12.800
Remember the internet and
the virtual spaces-- more

01:06:12.800 --> 01:06:15.560
people, more demands,
more noises, et cetera.

01:06:15.560 --> 01:06:19.580
Power and power differentials--
who's going to be at the table

01:06:19.580 --> 01:06:24.170
once we think about the new
energy management regimes?

01:06:24.170 --> 01:06:28.520
The Kyoto Protocol is
up for reconsideration

01:06:28.520 --> 01:06:31.410
I guess in six years or so.

01:06:31.410 --> 01:06:33.890
Well, who's going to be
sitting at that table other

01:06:33.890 --> 01:06:37.330
than countries?

01:06:37.330 --> 01:06:39.820
And then finally, the
issue of politics--

01:06:39.820 --> 01:06:41.830
we talk about energy policy.

01:06:41.830 --> 01:06:44.200
We do research on energy policy.

01:06:44.200 --> 01:06:48.250
We're beginning to think
about the politics associated

01:06:48.250 --> 01:06:51.340
with getting the legislature
to understand that these are

01:06:51.340 --> 01:06:54.280
important issues
or getting the EU

01:06:54.280 --> 01:06:58.270
or getting the
German chancellor who

01:06:58.270 --> 01:07:00.970
has been very successful
in the last few days

01:07:00.970 --> 01:07:05.950
to convince her colleagues
that the energy environment is

01:07:05.950 --> 01:07:08.860
an important global issue.

01:07:08.860 --> 01:07:11.750
From a political
scientist's point of view,

01:07:11.750 --> 01:07:14.990
the missing piece
constitutes very bad news.

01:07:14.990 --> 01:07:16.870
And so we have a
responsibility to try

01:07:16.870 --> 01:07:23.405
to factor in politics back into
the energy economy environment

01:07:23.405 --> 01:07:23.905
mix.

01:07:27.090 --> 01:07:31.565
Let me turn now to the
second item for today.

01:07:31.565 --> 01:07:33.690
There's a light here that's
going to start blinking

01:07:33.690 --> 01:07:34.690
when I should stop.

01:07:34.690 --> 01:07:39.180
So whenever it starts
blinking, I will stop.

01:07:39.180 --> 01:07:42.150
I'd like to give you some
sense of the global system

01:07:42.150 --> 01:07:45.000
today based on the
principle on what you see

01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:47.010
depends on how you look at it.

01:07:47.010 --> 01:07:49.080
Please take that
seriously, even though this

01:07:49.080 --> 01:07:53.100
sounds like a trite
observation because I

01:07:53.100 --> 01:07:56.100
will show you some
multiple realities,

01:07:56.100 --> 01:07:59.220
the size of those realities
on a global basis.

01:07:59.220 --> 01:08:01.200
And you can think of
this as footprints

01:08:01.200 --> 01:08:05.970
or as burdens or whatever, but
this is the world out there.

01:08:05.970 --> 01:08:10.530
We'll start with the relative
view of the people, the power,

01:08:10.530 --> 01:08:13.300
and the pain.

01:08:13.300 --> 01:08:14.910
This is the total
world population.

01:08:14.910 --> 01:08:17.370
There's a familiar
world map kind

01:08:17.370 --> 01:08:22.200
of adjusted a little bit to the
relative demographic statistics

01:08:22.200 --> 01:08:24.240
per country.

01:08:24.240 --> 01:08:28.229
And this is the same world
map adjusted a little bit

01:08:28.229 --> 01:08:36.510
to the same people but warring
from 1945 to two years ago.

01:08:36.510 --> 01:08:43.140
People at war, so to speak,
or populations at war.

01:08:43.140 --> 01:08:46.770
And this is the same
global map inflated

01:08:46.770 --> 01:08:53.213
by percentage of military
spending for the year 2002.

01:08:53.213 --> 01:08:54.630
Now the only purpose
of all this--

01:08:54.630 --> 01:08:57.720
there's a whole bunch of
that we will share with you--

01:08:57.720 --> 01:09:00.630
is that these are
conventional indicators.

01:09:00.630 --> 01:09:04.380
And hey, this is
the way life is.

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:10.830
But then we have some even
maybe conventional but seriously

01:09:10.830 --> 01:09:14.640
unpleasant situations
that if we can actually

01:09:14.640 --> 01:09:17.700
locate those landmines
and if we can seriously

01:09:17.700 --> 01:09:21.950
think about reconstructing those
areas in which the landmines

01:09:21.950 --> 01:09:26.359
were there, have
been put in place,

01:09:26.359 --> 01:09:28.939
what's the energy
strategy for doing this?

01:09:32.000 --> 01:09:35.270
The past has shown us that we
usually build what we already

01:09:35.270 --> 01:09:38.490
know how to build, which is
built into inefficiencies--

01:09:38.490 --> 01:09:41.520
what happens next.

01:09:41.520 --> 01:09:47.950
This is who died
violently, and this

01:09:47.950 --> 01:09:51.220
is who died through self-death.

01:09:51.220 --> 01:09:54.580
Now suicide is the word.

01:09:54.580 --> 01:09:56.530
In the social sciences,
we think of suicide

01:09:56.530 --> 01:09:58.960
as a reliable indicator
of unhappiness.

01:10:04.990 --> 01:10:07.580
This is at the individual level.

01:10:07.580 --> 01:10:10.630
But what happens
if we try to talk

01:10:10.630 --> 01:10:12.790
about aggregate unhappiness?

01:10:12.790 --> 01:10:17.890
And I'm not trying to draw
too much into this distortion.

01:10:17.890 --> 01:10:23.090
This is telling us that if we
violate the ecological fallacy,

01:10:23.090 --> 01:10:26.560
which is you can generalize from
the individual to the state,

01:10:26.560 --> 01:10:30.040
then we have sources of real
unhappiness in the globe

01:10:30.040 --> 01:10:36.130
that we haven't seriously
paid attention to.

01:10:36.130 --> 01:10:40.330
The next two slides are the
missing pieces of the migration

01:10:40.330 --> 01:10:42.220
that I showed you
a long time ago.

01:10:42.220 --> 01:10:44.980
And this is people leaving.

01:10:44.980 --> 01:10:46.630
Where are they leaving from?

01:10:46.630 --> 01:10:48.920
And they're not
leaving voluntarily.

01:10:48.920 --> 01:10:50.980
Now remember when
people cross borders,

01:10:50.980 --> 01:10:53.440
they come with their
claims and their demands

01:10:53.440 --> 01:10:58.430
on infrastructure,
including energy.

01:10:58.430 --> 01:11:05.200
So this is where they have
left from not voluntarily--

01:11:05.200 --> 01:11:06.650
because of conflict
and violence.

01:11:06.650 --> 01:11:09.430
And again, refugees are not part
of the migration statistics.

01:11:09.430 --> 01:11:11.760
It's a different category.

01:11:11.760 --> 01:11:14.270
And this is where they go to.

01:11:17.090 --> 01:11:20.750
The interesting thing about
this other than the most obvious

01:11:20.750 --> 01:11:27.850
is the mutual
concentration in an area

01:11:27.850 --> 01:11:33.265
which is Europe and Africa
and the rest of the globe.

01:11:35.830 --> 01:11:38.680
Well, we know
we're going to have

01:11:38.680 --> 01:11:42.820
under normal circumstances,
not refugee-related massive

01:11:42.820 --> 01:11:46.730
investments in infrastructure.

01:11:46.730 --> 01:11:49.210
Now, the good news--

01:11:49.210 --> 01:11:53.620
views of the world in
terms of human creativity

01:11:53.620 --> 01:11:57.730
using patents as an
example and internet usage

01:11:57.730 --> 01:12:01.420
indicating from my point of
view the ability for individuals

01:12:01.420 --> 01:12:05.630
to participate or to
express themselves.

01:12:05.630 --> 01:12:10.100
This is patents granted in 2002.

01:12:10.100 --> 01:12:12.680
Is there anything
odd about this map?

01:12:12.680 --> 01:12:16.220
Does anything strike you as not
the way we would wish it to be?

01:12:21.220 --> 01:12:25.710
There was a question earlier
to President Hockfield

01:12:25.710 --> 01:12:31.830
about the extent to which we
consider economic issues when

01:12:31.830 --> 01:12:37.830
we think about energy
and energy futures.

01:12:37.830 --> 01:12:40.710
We do consider them very much.

01:12:40.710 --> 01:12:43.530
Before seeing this
particular map,

01:12:43.530 --> 01:12:47.280
I had not expected
the dual balloon view.

01:12:47.280 --> 01:12:48.810
I'd expect the
concentration to be

01:12:48.810 --> 01:12:53.550
much more on the Western side.

01:12:53.550 --> 01:12:58.890
And this is internet
users in 2002.

01:12:58.890 --> 01:13:04.470
For me, internet users represent
voicing of individuals.

01:13:04.470 --> 01:13:06.420
It's the first time
in human history

01:13:06.420 --> 01:13:10.580
that an individual
unit, a statistic,

01:13:10.580 --> 01:13:16.250
as opposed to an aggregate,
is able to articulate anything

01:13:16.250 --> 01:13:21.870
on in any communication
venue on a global scale.

01:13:21.870 --> 01:13:27.020
So that's the other side
of the world that we live--

01:13:27.020 --> 01:13:30.100
another view other views of
the world that we live in.

01:13:33.600 --> 01:13:36.450
A couple more things before
we move on to the political

01:13:36.450 --> 01:13:40.220
realities-- think of these
are just descriptors--

01:13:40.220 --> 01:13:42.660
the past wealth, the
carbon emissions,

01:13:42.660 --> 01:13:44.900
and the future wealth.

01:13:44.900 --> 01:13:47.630
This is the wealth of the
globe at the beginning

01:13:47.630 --> 01:13:49.320
of the last century.

01:13:49.320 --> 01:13:51.170
This is the wealth,
the carbon emissions,

01:13:51.170 --> 01:13:52.740
and you see that's
not too difficult.

01:13:52.740 --> 01:13:59.180
And this is the wealth
of the globe in 2015.

01:13:59.180 --> 01:14:02.210
There's a big green
chunk in there

01:14:02.210 --> 01:14:05.560
that was not anticipated before.

01:14:05.560 --> 01:14:07.750
Now let's turn to
the real thing,

01:14:07.750 --> 01:14:11.920
which is the political
realities, the politics

01:14:11.920 --> 01:14:15.880
of energy for the 21st century.

01:14:15.880 --> 01:14:19.710
And only thing I will do is
highlight structural features--

01:14:19.710 --> 01:14:23.500
the parameters of the game
rather than policy features.

01:14:23.500 --> 01:14:26.080
What should we tweak
here and there?

01:14:26.080 --> 01:14:30.510
Again, this is what we
now have to live with.

01:14:30.510 --> 01:14:32.830
We have more global volatility.

01:14:32.830 --> 01:14:33.920
It goes without saying.

01:14:33.920 --> 01:14:35.840
We have more sovereign states.

01:14:35.840 --> 01:14:38.310
We have more
intergovernmental agencies.

01:14:38.310 --> 01:14:40.250
We have more multinational
corporations.

01:14:40.250 --> 01:14:42.470
That, I suppose,
is the good news.

01:14:42.470 --> 01:14:46.700
But we also have more
non-governmental organizations.

01:14:46.700 --> 01:14:49.250
Now the combination
of all these mores

01:14:49.250 --> 01:14:54.020
creates a very interesting
political algorithm.

01:14:54.020 --> 01:14:57.330
Just a quick view of
each one of those--

01:14:57.330 --> 01:15:01.010
the more nation-states,
more civil wars,

01:15:01.010 --> 01:15:05.810
and more terrorist rebel groups
in the very recent years.

01:15:05.810 --> 01:15:08.870
We call this volatility.

01:15:08.870 --> 01:15:10.850
This is the creation
of new states

01:15:10.850 --> 01:15:14.150
now think about new states as
every one of those entities

01:15:14.150 --> 01:15:16.190
has a vote--

01:15:16.190 --> 01:15:19.810
positive, negative, or neutral--
and is sitting at the table

01:15:19.810 --> 01:15:23.000
and is actually sitting
at all environment

01:15:23.000 --> 01:15:28.760
and all energy-related
negotiation tables.

01:15:28.760 --> 01:15:35.010
The larger the number of
individual decision entities,

01:15:35.010 --> 01:15:38.310
states, the more
complicated things become,

01:15:38.310 --> 01:15:41.100
but this is the
world we live in.

01:15:41.100 --> 01:15:44.760
The UN membership
more, the establishment

01:15:44.760 --> 01:15:47.070
of leading intergovernmental
organization,

01:15:47.070 --> 01:15:52.860
which is bureaucracies at
the global level, more.

01:15:52.860 --> 01:15:55.870
And then we have
more multinationals.

01:15:55.870 --> 01:15:59.350
And here the figures
speak for themselves.

01:15:59.350 --> 01:16:03.020
Let me draw your attention
to the very last bullet,

01:16:03.020 --> 01:16:09.330
and that is 50 of
the world's largest--

01:16:09.330 --> 01:16:14.670
50th largest economies
are not countries.

01:16:14.670 --> 01:16:17.380
They're corporations.

01:16:17.380 --> 01:16:20.050
So the economic size is massive.

01:16:20.050 --> 01:16:22.483
The political vote can
never be direct, of course,

01:16:22.483 --> 01:16:24.150
because corporations,
they'll sit around

01:16:24.150 --> 01:16:26.750
the table as individual voters.

01:16:26.750 --> 01:16:29.280
They sit around the table
as part of the lobbyists

01:16:29.280 --> 01:16:31.570
or the supporters of
individual states.

01:16:31.570 --> 01:16:33.070
And the difficulty
there, of course,

01:16:33.070 --> 01:16:36.000
is that the global corporations
have global interests that

01:16:36.000 --> 01:16:40.710
don't always map to those
of the individual states.

01:16:40.710 --> 01:16:49.380
And here is the really, really
newcomer of structural features

01:16:49.380 --> 01:16:54.690
of the world, and that is the
growth in non-governmental

01:16:54.690 --> 01:16:56.130
organizations--

01:16:56.130 --> 01:17:00.240
otherwise known as
NGOs, otherwise known

01:17:00.240 --> 01:17:04.890
as interest groups
that are organized

01:17:04.890 --> 01:17:07.050
to do good things in
the world, to do better

01:17:07.050 --> 01:17:08.580
in the world, et cetera.

01:17:08.580 --> 01:17:10.050
Two points are relevant here.

01:17:10.050 --> 01:17:13.920
One is they have actually
been able to secure

01:17:13.920 --> 01:17:18.410
a seat at the table
at the political table

01:17:18.410 --> 01:17:22.470
and international forums, not
voting, but they are there.

01:17:22.470 --> 01:17:26.340
And they make their views known.

01:17:26.340 --> 01:17:28.890
Second important thing
that's really important

01:17:28.890 --> 01:17:32.820
is that these are entities
without accountability.

01:17:32.820 --> 01:17:34.590
There is no mechanism
that has yet

01:17:34.590 --> 01:17:38.850
been invented to make these
entities accountable to anyone,

01:17:38.850 --> 01:17:41.880
really, except themselves if
they have an accountability

01:17:41.880 --> 01:17:42.880
mechanism.

01:17:42.880 --> 01:17:45.960
So we consider that important
and interesting because it's

01:17:45.960 --> 01:17:49.080
an example of incomplete
institutional design.

01:17:49.080 --> 01:17:54.020
It's very unlikely that the
situation will remain as it is.

01:17:54.020 --> 01:17:56.370
But because the
voices are so strong

01:17:56.370 --> 01:17:59.580
and they're very, very
strong and very organized,

01:17:59.580 --> 01:18:01.980
on both sides of
the energy issues--

01:18:01.980 --> 01:18:04.530
whatever the sides might be--

01:18:04.530 --> 01:18:09.900
that these are very
well worth watching.

01:18:09.900 --> 01:18:12.680
So what does all this mean?

01:18:12.680 --> 01:18:18.530
And what does all this mean if
we think about global energy

01:18:18.530 --> 01:18:21.740
strategies and global energy
situations for the remainder

01:18:21.740 --> 01:18:25.850
of the century in the
context of the priorities

01:18:25.850 --> 01:18:29.330
of the nation and the priorities
from a scientific point

01:18:29.330 --> 01:18:30.350
of view?

01:18:30.350 --> 01:18:32.760
What it means is
really quite simple.

01:18:32.760 --> 01:18:36.830
It's started to blink, so
I have to speed it up here.

01:18:36.830 --> 01:18:39.500
There are more stakes
and more stakeholders--

01:18:39.500 --> 01:18:42.320
please notice I didn't
use the word stock

01:18:42.320 --> 01:18:45.020
and stockholder's interests--

01:18:45.020 --> 01:18:49.040
more constraints on
decision, more possibilities

01:18:49.040 --> 01:18:52.260
for spoilers, and
then, of course,

01:18:52.260 --> 01:18:53.940
more limits on the use of force.

01:18:53.940 --> 01:18:55.790
There are some
things that we really

01:18:55.790 --> 01:18:59.600
can't use the traditional
instruments of violence that

01:18:59.600 --> 01:19:02.970
has been so effective
in the past.

01:19:02.970 --> 01:19:09.390
So the bottom line is
that we are looking for--

01:19:09.390 --> 01:19:13.400
there's more need of the
politics of consensus.

01:19:13.400 --> 01:19:16.460
Politics, by definition, is
about competing, competition,

01:19:16.460 --> 01:19:17.610
and about conflict.

01:19:17.610 --> 01:19:20.690
Now we're trying to figure
out how to push the envelope

01:19:20.690 --> 01:19:25.460
on the collaboration and on
the proverbial win-win-win-win

01:19:25.460 --> 01:19:26.510
situations.

01:19:26.510 --> 01:19:28.220
That is part of
actually thinking

01:19:28.220 --> 01:19:32.990
about new strategies
for negotiation,

01:19:32.990 --> 01:19:37.070
assuming we have developed
the national consensus on what

01:19:37.070 --> 01:19:37.950
we need to do.

01:19:37.950 --> 01:19:41.360
Now is not clear to me that we
have a national consensus yet,

01:19:41.360 --> 01:19:45.050
but let's assume
we'll have one soon.

01:19:45.050 --> 01:19:47.870
We then have to
move that consensus

01:19:47.870 --> 01:19:52.090
and try to influence
the global arena.

01:19:52.090 --> 01:19:53.630
If it's the politics
of consensus,

01:19:53.630 --> 01:19:56.300
that means let's
talk, and there are

01:19:56.300 --> 01:20:02.030
some barriers to being able to
speak clearly for this reason.

01:20:02.030 --> 01:20:08.160
And that is that on the one
hand, we're moving to a world

01:20:08.160 --> 01:20:12.660
where individuals and peoples
begin to matter, that peoples

01:20:12.660 --> 01:20:14.232
are not always
willing to be funneled

01:20:14.232 --> 01:20:15.690
through their
national governments.

01:20:15.690 --> 01:20:18.090
Every one of the governments,
of course, speaks English,

01:20:18.090 --> 01:20:21.070
so this is not relevant to them.

01:20:21.070 --> 01:20:23.310
But on the other hand,
we've got to figure out

01:20:23.310 --> 01:20:26.260
how to reduce the
gap in communication

01:20:26.260 --> 01:20:31.320
and in the disconnects in
our respective communications

01:20:31.320 --> 01:20:36.450
of which language is one of
the more pervasive barriers.

01:20:36.450 --> 01:20:41.220
So conclusion-- I
have two conclusions.

01:20:41.220 --> 01:20:49.780
So conclusion number one
is restating the obvious.

01:20:49.780 --> 01:20:53.410
I think, okay, okay, okay, okay.

01:20:53.410 --> 01:20:54.100
Be very fast.

01:20:57.240 --> 01:21:00.190
The second bullet, we have
to pay an inheritance tax.

01:21:00.190 --> 01:21:01.940
We've got this legacy.

01:21:01.940 --> 01:21:03.220
Let's pay the tax.

01:21:03.220 --> 01:21:05.380
Not let's pay-- we're
going to have to.

01:21:05.380 --> 01:21:08.770
And we can minimize the pain.

01:21:08.770 --> 01:21:10.070
This is my last slide.

01:21:10.070 --> 01:21:15.190
So allow me to take it slowly,
because it's rather important.

01:21:15.190 --> 01:21:17.290
We've learned a couple
of things from the legacy

01:21:17.290 --> 01:21:21.610
of the 20th century wait, okay.

01:21:21.610 --> 01:21:25.460
One is context matters,
et cetera, et cetera.

01:21:25.460 --> 01:21:28.120
The one that is most serious--

01:21:28.120 --> 01:21:30.160
this is my last
sentence, I promise--

01:21:30.160 --> 01:21:32.440
the most serious from
an analytical point

01:21:32.440 --> 01:21:34.910
of view, challenging from
an analytical point of view,

01:21:34.910 --> 01:21:37.570
most challenging and one that
we're trying to deal with

01:21:37.570 --> 01:21:38.980
is the third bullet.

01:21:38.980 --> 01:21:42.190
And that is to take into
account the time factor.

01:21:42.190 --> 01:21:42.818
Time is money.

01:21:42.818 --> 01:21:43.360
We know that.

01:21:43.360 --> 01:21:44.992
And time marches along somehow.

01:21:44.992 --> 01:21:45.700
We know that too.

01:21:45.700 --> 01:21:48.690
But what else this time do?

01:21:48.690 --> 01:21:52.403
Now it's your turn to
answer the question.

01:21:52.403 --> 01:21:53.882
Thank you.

01:21:53.882 --> 01:22:00.291
[APPLAUSE]

01:22:04.740 --> 01:22:05.530
CHOUCRI: I do?

01:22:05.530 --> 01:22:06.030
Oh.

01:22:09.490 --> 01:22:11.462
So apparently, there
is time for questions.

01:22:20.620 --> 01:22:21.600
Yep.

01:22:21.600 --> 01:22:25.100
AUDIENCE: Hello,
I believe that we

01:22:25.100 --> 01:22:28.780
are in an emergency situation
as we speak right now.

01:22:28.780 --> 01:22:32.190
And I define emergency as
when you see the bullet coming

01:22:32.190 --> 01:22:34.530
at you, and you know
there's not enough time

01:22:34.530 --> 01:22:36.900
to get out of the way.

01:22:36.900 --> 01:22:41.170
In particular, I think the
emergency is one of oil.

01:22:41.170 --> 01:22:44.430
I've seen recent articles
in the Wall Street Journal

01:22:44.430 --> 01:22:48.540
where there was a clarion cry by
several accounts for large oil

01:22:48.540 --> 01:22:49.170
companies.

01:22:49.170 --> 01:22:51.720
I've spoken privately
with someone

01:22:51.720 --> 01:22:54.480
who was on the board of a major
oil company in the last five

01:22:54.480 --> 01:22:55.570
years.

01:22:55.570 --> 01:22:57.540
There was also a
public statement

01:22:57.540 --> 01:23:03.270
made by the CEO of Total S.A.
That there was no possible way

01:23:03.270 --> 01:23:08.490
that we can meet the
projected growth and the oil

01:23:08.490 --> 01:23:10.630
requirements of the world.

01:23:10.630 --> 01:23:14.910
And I believe that we're looking
at a permanent and growing

01:23:14.910 --> 01:23:20.310
worldwide oil shortage in
approximately 5 to 10 years,

01:23:20.310 --> 01:23:23.730
and we're out of time where
changing out the transportation

01:23:23.730 --> 01:23:26.070
fleet takes about 20 years.

01:23:26.070 --> 01:23:29.040
So my questions
to you are, first,

01:23:29.040 --> 01:23:34.480
is this recognized in
the energy staff of MIT?

01:23:34.480 --> 01:23:40.320
Two, given that
this would be true,

01:23:40.320 --> 01:23:42.600
what are the implications
for the economy

01:23:42.600 --> 01:23:46.030
and political structure and
stability of the United States?

01:23:46.030 --> 01:23:48.580
And three, what are the
implications for world

01:23:48.580 --> 01:23:50.790
war in a situation like this?

01:23:53.880 --> 01:23:58.130
The answer to the first question
is is, yes, it's recognized,

01:23:58.130 --> 01:24:01.340
and that's a very
important footnote here.

01:24:01.340 --> 01:24:05.430
For those of us that were
members of the faculty in 1973

01:24:05.430 --> 01:24:09.050
when the first oil crisis--
the first big one--

01:24:09.050 --> 01:24:11.900
the Institute's response was
the establishment of the Energy

01:24:11.900 --> 01:24:13.040
Lab, if you recall.

01:24:15.950 --> 01:24:20.390
Several of us were talking
the other day about the very

01:24:20.390 --> 01:24:23.390
unfortunate deja vu image.

01:24:23.390 --> 01:24:27.290
We've gone through this before.

01:24:27.290 --> 01:24:30.820
If we didn't know that
this was 2000-something,

01:24:30.820 --> 01:24:34.760
it could have been
1973 with respect

01:24:34.760 --> 01:24:39.000
to oil in a very narrow way.

01:24:39.000 --> 01:24:41.270
That particular
crisis disappeared.

01:24:41.270 --> 01:24:46.070
As you remember, the decade of
the '80s, things went smoother.

01:24:46.070 --> 01:24:51.380
So I would say it's a little
worse than you have described

01:24:51.380 --> 01:24:56.780
it because, now, not only do
we have this oil business--

01:24:56.780 --> 01:25:00.740
and there hasn't been any
good news on the oil front

01:25:00.740 --> 01:25:02.090
for quite a while--

01:25:02.090 --> 01:25:05.680
I didn't show you the real scary
part, which is the refinery.

01:25:11.030 --> 01:25:14.330
The refineries are operating
almost at full capacity,

01:25:14.330 --> 01:25:16.460
and that as they say
in very good news

01:25:16.460 --> 01:25:18.590
just pushing on
the infrastructure.

01:25:18.590 --> 01:25:20.870
In addition to this, we
have all this other stuff

01:25:20.870 --> 01:25:23.810
that's come along, which is
the CO2 stuff, which was not

01:25:23.810 --> 01:25:29.600
part of the 1973 oil crisis.

01:25:29.600 --> 01:25:31.820
We didn't recognize
it at the time.

01:25:31.820 --> 01:25:37.340
With respect to world
war, the core of what I do

01:25:37.340 --> 01:25:39.300
is conflict and violence.

01:25:39.300 --> 01:25:41.120
Why do people go to war?

01:25:41.120 --> 01:25:43.080
And when is this going
to happen, et cetera?

01:25:43.080 --> 01:25:44.480
So I expect war any time.

01:25:47.810 --> 01:25:50.380
I think I that I do not
want to get into this.

01:25:55.303 --> 01:25:56.720
I also teach Middle
East politics,

01:25:56.720 --> 01:25:57.720
so you can imagine how--

01:26:01.580 --> 01:26:02.080
that's it?

01:26:02.080 --> 01:26:03.330
MODERATOR: One more over here.

01:26:03.330 --> 01:26:04.260
AUDIENCE: I have one.

01:26:04.260 --> 01:26:05.450
Am I on?

01:26:05.450 --> 01:26:06.640
CHOUCRI: Okay.

01:26:06.640 --> 01:26:07.610
Yeah.

01:26:07.610 --> 01:26:19.750
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]

01:26:19.750 --> 01:26:24.738
CHOUCRI: Well, Iraq's
oil is not flowing.

01:26:24.738 --> 01:26:28.706
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
it's the whole region--

01:26:28.706 --> 01:26:31.424
Iraq, Iran [INAUDIBLE]

01:26:31.424 --> 01:26:33.210
CHOUCRI: Let me say
the premise first.

01:26:33.210 --> 01:26:41.580
It's not clear to me that the US
presence is a major contributor

01:26:41.580 --> 01:26:46.200
to stability in the region
because the instabilities don't

01:26:46.200 --> 01:26:47.580
come from governments anymore.

01:26:47.580 --> 01:26:53.300
They come from groups, people,
terrorist, good guy, whatever.

01:26:53.300 --> 01:26:55.380
The real instability
is happening

01:26:55.380 --> 01:26:58.750
because governments are losing
control over their people--

01:26:58.750 --> 01:27:00.760
so with or without
the US troops there.

01:27:00.760 --> 01:27:05.310
And unless the US is going
to try, which is impossible,

01:27:05.310 --> 01:27:07.890
my colleagues tell me,
because of the constraints,

01:27:07.890 --> 01:27:13.050
the limits on manpower in
the military to really police

01:27:13.050 --> 01:27:14.800
the entire region.

01:27:14.800 --> 01:27:18.420
So the bottom line is
it's not clear to me

01:27:18.420 --> 01:27:22.860
whether the physical presence
contributes to stability

01:27:22.860 --> 01:27:26.790
or fuels instability.

01:27:26.790 --> 01:27:28.580
AUDIENCE: Hi, can you hear me?

01:27:28.580 --> 01:27:29.610
CHOUCRI: Yeah.

01:27:29.610 --> 01:27:32.183
AUDIENCE: Okay, I
thought you said

01:27:32.183 --> 01:27:34.600
a couple of really good things,
and I want to repeat them.

01:27:34.600 --> 01:27:37.000
CHOUCRI: Thank you.

01:27:37.000 --> 01:27:39.390
AUDIENCE: And then I have
sort of a general concern,

01:27:39.390 --> 01:27:40.720
but I won't really name that.

01:27:40.720 --> 01:27:43.270
And I'm going to ask
a specific question.

01:27:43.270 --> 01:27:46.180
So one of the big
factors in my life--

01:27:46.180 --> 01:27:47.700
and maybe it sounds
like in yours--

01:27:47.700 --> 01:27:51.120
is to help people talk
better about politics.

01:27:51.120 --> 01:27:54.920
Because as you mentioned, people
find it very uncomfortable.

01:27:54.920 --> 01:27:56.370
And unless you're
a professional,

01:27:56.370 --> 01:27:58.230
people almost don't
want to do it.

01:27:58.230 --> 01:28:00.233
So you're one of our few.

01:28:00.233 --> 01:28:01.150
I'm on the other side.

01:28:01.150 --> 01:28:02.108
I'm not a professional.

01:28:02.108 --> 01:28:03.077
I never want to be.

01:28:03.077 --> 01:28:04.660
But I want to talk
about it, so I want

01:28:04.660 --> 01:28:06.512
to learn how to talk about it.

01:28:06.512 --> 01:28:08.470
So the other thing you
said that I really liked

01:28:08.470 --> 01:28:14.020
was that it was on this slide
about the world languages.

01:28:14.020 --> 01:28:16.390
And you said-- let me find
the quote-- here it is.

01:28:16.390 --> 01:28:20.270
There are some barriers
to speaking clearly.

01:28:20.270 --> 01:28:24.340
And I think that sentence to me
encapsulates the entire problem

01:28:24.340 --> 01:28:25.850
of talking about politics.

01:28:25.850 --> 01:28:29.020
There are so many
barriers to clarity.

01:28:29.020 --> 01:28:33.010
And in that vein,
I'd like to ask

01:28:33.010 --> 01:28:38.320
for clarity on your
numerous pictures

01:28:38.320 --> 01:28:43.690
that showed the pretty
graphics of the world shapes.

01:28:43.690 --> 01:28:48.520
And I didn't know what
the size of the country

01:28:48.520 --> 01:28:50.870
meant as opposed to
like, for example,

01:28:50.870 --> 01:28:53.970
it seems like there are borders.

01:28:53.970 --> 01:28:56.320
They're always there
and no matter what.

01:28:56.320 --> 01:28:59.140
And the colors also change.

01:28:59.140 --> 01:29:01.780
And I wasn't sure what color was
what because it didn't clearly

01:29:01.780 --> 01:29:03.190
represent a rainbow to me.

01:29:05.860 --> 01:29:07.090
CHOUCRI: Yeah.

01:29:07.090 --> 01:29:09.090
MODERATOR: That was
the last question.

01:29:09.090 --> 01:29:11.760
CHOUCRI: First of
all, I apologize

01:29:11.760 --> 01:29:15.780
for not being clear at the very
beginning about those maps.

01:29:15.780 --> 01:29:18.090
This is a representation.

01:29:18.090 --> 01:29:21.750
These were distortions of
the territory-based maps

01:29:21.750 --> 01:29:23.070
that we're familiar with.

01:29:23.070 --> 01:29:27.610
Pick up any atlas, you see
the political boundaries.

01:29:27.610 --> 01:29:29.790
So it's just readjusting
the boundaries

01:29:29.790 --> 01:29:33.480
by the relative size
of the US population

01:29:33.480 --> 01:29:36.558
relative to the
total population.

01:29:36.558 --> 01:29:38.850
The colors were not meant to
be happy colors, rainbows.

01:29:38.850 --> 01:29:41.520
It just were just those colors.

01:29:41.520 --> 01:29:44.680
And about talking
about politics,

01:29:44.680 --> 01:29:45.930
I only do it in class.

01:29:45.930 --> 01:29:47.610
I can't give you any advice.

01:29:47.610 --> 01:29:51.480
And my last statement is this.

01:29:51.480 --> 01:29:55.930
It's in the form of a PR for
the research that we're doing,

01:29:55.930 --> 01:29:58.050
but it's rather
interesting or important.

01:29:58.050 --> 01:30:01.800
And that is the global system
for sustainable development

01:30:01.800 --> 01:30:03.450
is a knowledge
networking system,

01:30:03.450 --> 01:30:07.080
distributed system that
is mirror sited in China

01:30:07.080 --> 01:30:12.650
and currently going to be
mirror sited in Lebanon.

01:30:12.650 --> 01:30:17.400
It operates in four
languages, two of which

01:30:17.400 --> 01:30:20.070
are not really your
common normal languages

01:30:20.070 --> 01:30:23.100
of everyday discourse
in the United States--

01:30:23.100 --> 01:30:28.350
Arabic and Chinese, English,
of course, and the fourth

01:30:28.350 --> 01:30:31.770
is French.

01:30:31.770 --> 01:30:36.270
And given the fact that the
world's population is not

01:30:36.270 --> 01:30:38.610
English speaking,
we're very pleased

01:30:38.610 --> 01:30:43.470
and very proud to be able
to operate in at least two

01:30:43.470 --> 01:30:45.990
of the major
non-Western languages

01:30:45.990 --> 01:30:48.780
where we know there
are lots of people

01:30:48.780 --> 01:30:50.940
that don't speak English.

01:30:50.940 --> 01:30:56.210
So if you have a chance, take
a look at the GSSD website

01:30:56.210 --> 01:30:59.950
because this is a
typical MIT product.

01:30:59.950 --> 01:31:01.860
And what is not typical
is that it comes out

01:31:01.860 --> 01:31:03.420
of the social
sciences as opposed

01:31:03.420 --> 01:31:05.224
to the rest of the campus.

01:31:05.224 --> 01:31:06.655
Thank you.

01:31:06.655 --> 01:31:13.340
[APPLAUSE]

01:31:14.910 --> 01:31:16.590
HOCHMAN: Well, I'd
like to say that I'm

01:31:16.590 --> 01:31:20.010
thrilled and privileged to
introduce Professor John

01:31:20.010 --> 01:31:21.060
Fernandez.

01:31:21.060 --> 01:31:23.280
Part of the reason
I'm thrilled is

01:31:23.280 --> 01:31:25.680
because as of about
8:00 PM last night,

01:31:25.680 --> 01:31:30.840
he was trapped in the
New Orleans airport.

01:31:30.840 --> 01:31:36.990
And despite FAA computer crashes
and tropical weather systems,

01:31:36.990 --> 01:31:39.750
he managed to make
his way here today.

01:31:39.750 --> 01:31:44.430
So I really am thrilled to be
able to introduce Professor

01:31:44.430 --> 01:31:46.500
John Fernandez.

01:31:46.500 --> 01:31:48.840
An associate professor
of building technology

01:31:48.840 --> 01:31:52.170
in the MIT department of
architecture and a practicing

01:31:52.170 --> 01:31:54.720
architect, Professor
Fernandez primarily

01:31:54.720 --> 01:31:58.500
focuses his research on
the materials of buildings

01:31:58.500 --> 01:32:02.130
and the design of assemblies
for resource efficient

01:32:02.130 --> 01:32:03.637
built environment.

01:32:03.637 --> 01:32:05.970
Lately, his interests have
been centered on the emerging

01:32:05.970 --> 01:32:10.020
opportunities for linking
design, urban planning,

01:32:10.020 --> 01:32:13.440
and building technologies in
the creation of green cities

01:32:13.440 --> 01:32:15.930
and resilient communities.

01:32:15.930 --> 01:32:17.370
This morning,
Professor Fernandez

01:32:17.370 --> 01:32:18.840
will address the
growing challenge

01:32:18.840 --> 01:32:23.880
of limited global resources amid
an ever-expanding urban world.

01:32:23.880 --> 01:32:27.488
Please join me in welcoming
Professor John Fernandez.

01:32:27.488 --> 01:32:34.236
[APPLAUSE]

01:32:36.660 --> 01:32:38.270
FERNANDEZ: Can you hear me?

01:32:38.270 --> 01:32:39.560
Mic's on.

01:32:39.560 --> 01:32:42.550
I have to do a little
A/V shift here.

01:32:42.550 --> 01:32:45.217
The A/V person
asked me to do this.

01:32:45.217 --> 01:32:46.050
I think I can do it.

01:32:46.050 --> 01:32:50.680
Although I'm MIT faculty, so
it might take a little bit.

01:32:50.680 --> 01:32:51.180
There we go.

01:32:56.603 --> 01:33:00.570
Okay, yes, I was in
New Orleans yesterday.

01:33:00.570 --> 01:33:01.710
I flew in this morning.

01:33:04.580 --> 01:33:09.210
I did have a beautiful
black suit picked out.

01:33:09.210 --> 01:33:10.770
It's at home.

01:33:10.770 --> 01:33:12.375
I do bike into work
every day, so I

01:33:12.375 --> 01:33:13.500
rummaged through my office.

01:33:13.500 --> 01:33:14.820
And I found this.

01:33:14.820 --> 01:33:19.260
So please bear with me.

01:33:19.260 --> 01:33:22.107
I'm thrilled to be here.

01:33:22.107 --> 01:33:23.940
I am part of the
department of architecture.

01:33:23.940 --> 01:33:28.830
We're a little department at
MIT just across the street.

01:33:28.830 --> 01:33:34.900
I'm class of '85 from that
department and back as faculty.

01:33:34.900 --> 01:33:42.150
Thank you to Ira, to the Tech
Day committee for inviting me.

01:33:42.150 --> 01:33:48.660
I just want to take a minute
and thank the class of 1957,

01:33:48.660 --> 01:33:50.340
not just because
it is your 50th,

01:33:50.340 --> 01:33:54.450
but because that class was
very generous in supporting

01:33:54.450 --> 01:33:57.870
my work the last few years as
a career development professor.

01:33:57.870 --> 01:34:02.260
So I really very
much appreciate that.

01:34:02.260 --> 01:34:05.080
Clearly, the topic of resources
and energy in particular

01:34:05.080 --> 01:34:07.107
is the topic of the day.

01:34:07.107 --> 01:34:09.190
It's going to be the topic
of generations to come.

01:34:09.190 --> 01:34:11.110
That's for certain.

01:34:11.110 --> 01:34:13.840
I'm trained as an architect, so
my expertise is in buildings.

01:34:13.840 --> 01:34:18.070
But in the last few years,
I've spent quite a lot of time

01:34:18.070 --> 01:34:24.110
on linking resources and design
and engineering of buildings.

01:34:24.110 --> 01:34:27.070
If you want to pick one
of Professor Deutsch's

01:34:27.070 --> 01:34:33.340
has strategies for dealing
with the energy issue,

01:34:33.340 --> 01:34:37.540
my talk would probably fall
under moderate demand--

01:34:37.540 --> 01:34:42.130
how to design better buildings
and how to design them in a way

01:34:42.130 --> 01:34:45.170
that people reduce their demand.

01:34:45.170 --> 01:34:46.670
In last several
years, I've actually

01:34:46.670 --> 01:34:50.940
spent quite a bit of time with
a variety of organizations,

01:34:50.940 --> 01:34:55.460
including architectural design
firms, various companies, now

01:34:55.460 --> 01:34:57.090
several cities.

01:34:57.090 --> 01:35:00.800
And I'm coming to the conclusion
that the momentum is there.

01:35:00.800 --> 01:35:03.290
It's not political momentum.

01:35:03.290 --> 01:35:07.730
It is grassroots momentum for
a better built environment.

01:35:10.670 --> 01:35:13.090
I like organized presentations.

01:35:13.090 --> 01:35:16.720
I'm a bit compulsive.

01:35:16.720 --> 01:35:19.310
This is organized
around three questions.

01:35:19.310 --> 01:35:21.610
Okay, first, it is
really important

01:35:21.610 --> 01:35:24.730
to be very clear about what the
built environment is, what am I

01:35:24.730 --> 01:35:28.870
talking about, and I'll
delve into that second

01:35:28.870 --> 01:35:31.030
is how much of our
global resources

01:35:31.030 --> 01:35:34.690
are consumed by
that environment,

01:35:34.690 --> 01:35:40.620
not only in operation, but also
in construction and demolition.

01:35:40.620 --> 01:35:44.220
And given what we know from
answers from the first two

01:35:44.220 --> 01:35:47.490
questions, should
we even change?

01:35:47.490 --> 01:35:48.540
Do we need to change?

01:35:48.540 --> 01:35:50.040
Will change in the
built environment

01:35:50.040 --> 01:35:53.670
make much of a difference?

01:35:53.670 --> 01:35:55.650
I'm going to throw out
a lot of information,

01:35:55.650 --> 01:35:56.760
lots of pretty pictures.

01:35:56.760 --> 01:35:58.110
I am an architect.

01:35:58.110 --> 01:36:02.010
So I'm hoping that you
can make your own decision

01:36:02.010 --> 01:36:05.370
about whether the built
environment usually left out

01:36:05.370 --> 01:36:07.320
of the discussion on energy--

01:36:07.320 --> 01:36:09.270
I should say often left out--

01:36:09.270 --> 01:36:11.460
should be part of
the core discussion

01:36:11.460 --> 01:36:17.330
of energy and global
climate change issues.

01:36:17.330 --> 01:36:21.360
So let's see what we can
get out of this effort.

01:36:21.360 --> 01:36:23.490
I have 40 minutes.

01:36:23.490 --> 01:36:27.030
I'm hitting a real brick wall
in terms of the schedule,

01:36:27.030 --> 01:36:30.090
and that's lunch at one o'clock.

01:36:30.090 --> 01:36:36.160
So I'll make sure to try to
get you to your lunch on time.

01:36:36.160 --> 01:36:38.250
Okay, so what's the
built environment?

01:36:38.250 --> 01:36:40.680
Let's revert to
some fundamentals.

01:36:40.680 --> 01:36:42.570
The built environment
very directly

01:36:42.570 --> 01:36:45.180
stated is that which
provides shelter,

01:36:45.180 --> 01:36:46.800
always has provided shelter.

01:36:46.800 --> 01:36:49.200
It's one of three very
basic fundamental needs

01:36:49.200 --> 01:36:52.050
of the human species, food
and water being the other two.

01:36:52.050 --> 01:36:53.910
There are very, very
few places on Earth

01:36:53.910 --> 01:36:57.390
where you can live
without shelter.

01:36:57.390 --> 01:36:59.850
The critical role of
shelters hasn't changed,

01:36:59.850 --> 01:37:04.200
but the diversity of
shelters, buildings

01:37:04.200 --> 01:37:08.820
has changed as our activities
have evolved and become

01:37:08.820 --> 01:37:09.930
much, much more diverse.

01:37:09.930 --> 01:37:12.222
So here this is actually a
photograph of a new building

01:37:12.222 --> 01:37:15.757
here in Boston, the Institute
of Contemporary Arts.

01:37:15.757 --> 01:37:17.340
When shelter was
first invented, there

01:37:17.340 --> 01:37:19.050
was no such thing as
contemporary arts.

01:37:19.050 --> 01:37:23.730
So the diversity of activities
has really exploded,

01:37:23.730 --> 01:37:25.230
and that's why the
built environment

01:37:25.230 --> 01:37:26.397
looks the way it does today.

01:37:28.950 --> 01:37:31.920
Our first understanding
of the world

01:37:31.920 --> 01:37:33.738
was very much about
where you could live

01:37:33.738 --> 01:37:35.530
and where you couldn't,
what was habitable,

01:37:35.530 --> 01:37:37.520
and what was inhabitable.

01:37:37.520 --> 01:37:39.330
And creating shelter
that allowed migration

01:37:39.330 --> 01:37:43.420
and settlement was the beginning
of the making of the largest,

01:37:43.420 --> 01:37:46.860
longest lasting most
material intensive,

01:37:46.860 --> 01:37:48.840
and now one of the most
energy intensive things

01:37:48.840 --> 01:37:51.190
we've ever made as a species.

01:37:51.190 --> 01:37:55.700
And that's what I want to
call the built environment.

01:37:55.700 --> 01:37:58.070
This is actually an image
from a colleague of mine Ann

01:37:58.070 --> 01:38:02.580
Spurn in architecture.

01:38:02.580 --> 01:38:05.660
Over time, very slowly at first,
domestication agriculture--

01:38:05.660 --> 01:38:06.920
we all know this story--

01:38:06.920 --> 01:38:10.250
along with a building arts led
to the creation of settlements.

01:38:10.250 --> 01:38:12.290
And this has not
abated ever since.

01:38:12.290 --> 01:38:14.870
Huge proportions of our
extracted material and energy

01:38:14.870 --> 01:38:19.100
resources have been devoted
to the making of buildings,

01:38:19.100 --> 01:38:22.280
settlements, roads, dams--
all the infrastructure

01:38:22.280 --> 01:38:28.820
that we depend on so that
today, this is what we have.

01:38:28.820 --> 01:38:31.150
This is the built environment.

01:38:31.150 --> 01:38:32.950
This could be many
different cities.

01:38:32.950 --> 01:38:35.348
Concrete is the
primary material used

01:38:35.348 --> 01:38:36.640
to build the built environment.

01:38:36.640 --> 01:38:41.870
It's actually the second most
consumed material in the world,

01:38:41.870 --> 01:38:44.980
second only to water by people.

01:38:44.980 --> 01:38:46.198
And actually, concrete also--

01:38:46.198 --> 01:38:47.740
I don't have this
in my script here--

01:38:47.740 --> 01:38:51.400
but concrete contributes some
somewhere between 6% and 8%

01:38:51.400 --> 01:38:54.110
of all CO2 emissions--

01:38:54.110 --> 01:38:56.843
so 6% to 8% of
total CO2 emissions.

01:39:02.020 --> 01:39:06.280
Let me give you some facts on
where we are with the built

01:39:06.280 --> 01:39:09.290
environment on a global scale.

01:39:09.290 --> 01:39:12.500
So within the last year,
this past year or 18 months,

01:39:12.500 --> 01:39:13.220
or two years--

01:39:13.220 --> 01:39:15.100
it depends on who
you're talking to--

01:39:15.100 --> 01:39:17.860
the global urban population
has surpassed 50%.

01:39:17.860 --> 01:39:20.040
Now 50% of the world
lives in cities,

01:39:20.040 --> 01:39:22.240
and that will
continue to increase.

01:39:22.240 --> 01:39:24.940
Projections say
pretty much forever,

01:39:24.940 --> 01:39:26.360
but that's predicting
the future.

01:39:26.360 --> 01:39:28.700
I won't be here in 75 years.

01:39:28.700 --> 01:39:30.910
So it's an easy
prediction to make.

01:39:30.910 --> 01:39:32.650
A majority of that
urban population's

01:39:32.650 --> 01:39:35.980
within easy reach of the coast.

01:39:35.980 --> 01:39:38.920
The fastest growing
urban populations by far

01:39:38.920 --> 01:39:43.750
are located in Asia and
Africa, and China now

01:39:43.750 --> 01:39:46.450
astoundingly accounts for
one half of all construction

01:39:46.450 --> 01:39:48.760
in the world.

01:39:48.760 --> 01:39:51.820
As a result, there are
significant resource issues

01:39:51.820 --> 01:39:52.930
to consider.

01:39:52.930 --> 01:39:56.560
80% of CO2 emissions is
caused directly or indirectly

01:39:56.560 --> 01:39:59.420
by people living in cities.

01:39:59.420 --> 01:40:02.470
75% of wood consumption is
used for construction and fuel

01:40:02.470 --> 01:40:04.510
in cities, and 60%
of water withdrawals

01:40:04.510 --> 01:40:10.290
also occurs to serve
the residents of cities.

01:40:10.290 --> 01:40:17.130
This is an urban world that
is also a very poor world,

01:40:17.130 --> 01:40:19.090
and we should take note--

01:40:19.090 --> 01:40:21.980
although I won't go into
that discussion today--

01:40:21.980 --> 01:40:26.510
that that's a really
significant issue

01:40:26.510 --> 01:40:31.410
because that level of affluence
will most likely increase.

01:40:31.410 --> 01:40:34.620
And that's a big
factor that we will

01:40:34.620 --> 01:40:36.960
have to take in terms
of resource consumption

01:40:36.960 --> 01:40:39.020
in the future cities.

01:40:39.020 --> 01:40:42.590
On the homefront in the
US, the built environment

01:40:42.590 --> 01:40:45.950
amounts to 130 million
residential buildings.

01:40:45.950 --> 01:40:50.480
We add approximately 1.8
million residents annually

01:40:50.480 --> 01:40:54.170
depending on the economy, six
million commercial buildings,

01:40:54.170 --> 01:40:57.620
170,000 new buildings
of this type annually.

01:40:57.620 --> 01:41:00.020
And we demolish about
50,000 buildings a year--

01:41:00.020 --> 01:41:03.710
commercial buildings a
year-- that again also varies

01:41:03.710 --> 01:41:07.350
significantly with the economy.

01:41:07.350 --> 01:41:11.520
The bad news is that
cities tend to promote

01:41:11.520 --> 01:41:13.710
more intense
resource consumption

01:41:13.710 --> 01:41:15.240
on a per capita basis.

01:41:15.240 --> 01:41:18.030
It's partly due because
the logistics of housing

01:41:18.030 --> 01:41:20.520
large numbers of people,
getting them to work,

01:41:20.520 --> 01:41:23.130
having places for them to
work, industries located

01:41:23.130 --> 01:41:26.520
around cities, but
also the affluence

01:41:26.520 --> 01:41:29.730
that's gained from living in
cities and working in cities

01:41:29.730 --> 01:41:32.040
and shopping in cities.

01:41:32.040 --> 01:41:34.050
Take, for example, the
increasing popularity

01:41:34.050 --> 01:41:36.558
of air conditioners in
cities around the world.

01:41:36.558 --> 01:41:38.100
Now I want to pause
here for a second

01:41:38.100 --> 01:41:41.340
and say that I'm part of a group
called the building technology

01:41:41.340 --> 01:41:42.690
program.

01:41:42.690 --> 01:41:46.410
And the people standing
behind me for now

01:41:46.410 --> 01:41:49.770
are Marilyn Anderson, John
Ochsendorf, Les Norford,

01:41:49.770 --> 01:41:51.330
and Leon Glicksman.

01:41:51.330 --> 01:41:53.140
We do all of this work together.

01:41:53.140 --> 01:41:54.780
And these air
conditioning numbers

01:41:54.780 --> 01:41:57.390
are something that
Leon has talked

01:41:57.390 --> 01:42:00.150
about for quite some time,
and I've taken them to heart.

01:42:00.150 --> 01:42:03.030
In China, more than 87 out
of 100 urban households

01:42:03.030 --> 01:42:07.387
now own an air conditioner
compared to 24 out of 102,000

01:42:07.387 --> 01:42:10.350
seven years ago.

01:42:10.350 --> 01:42:12.810
As a result, China is now
the world's largest producer

01:42:12.810 --> 01:42:16.560
of air conditioners, and India
is following closely behind.

01:42:16.560 --> 01:42:18.480
Now air conditioning
is something

01:42:18.480 --> 01:42:21.540
that a great deal of
the rest of the world

01:42:21.540 --> 01:42:24.500
is looking forward to.

01:42:24.500 --> 01:42:26.480
This is happening now
and gaining momentum

01:42:26.480 --> 01:42:32.630
and will continue to happen most
furiously in expanding cities.

01:42:32.630 --> 01:42:41.240
The image I'm showing here
is actually a per capita

01:42:41.240 --> 01:42:43.820
electricity consumption.

01:42:43.820 --> 01:42:46.190
Just notice the uptick--

01:42:46.190 --> 01:42:54.050
sorry-- the uptick in two-year
period in Shanghai, 1995, 1997,

01:42:54.050 --> 01:42:56.240
massive urbanization
liberalization of the housing

01:42:56.240 --> 01:43:00.020
market, increasing number
of air conditioners,

01:43:00.020 --> 01:43:03.620
and general affluence
increasing during that time.

01:43:03.620 --> 01:43:07.740
It's a 76% increase in
electrical consumption

01:43:07.740 --> 01:43:10.260
in that city over
a two-year period.

01:43:10.260 --> 01:43:14.730
Shanghai, by the way, for
the past several years--

01:43:14.730 --> 01:43:17.280
looks like next year
might be the same--

01:43:17.280 --> 01:43:21.720
has added more building
space than exists

01:43:21.720 --> 01:43:23.630
in all of Manhattan.

01:43:23.630 --> 01:43:25.590
That's every year.

01:43:25.590 --> 01:43:27.210
So let's do something here.

01:43:27.210 --> 01:43:29.640
Let's generalize these ideas.

01:43:29.640 --> 01:43:31.300
Let's kind of
bring them together

01:43:31.300 --> 01:43:34.000
because there is a
really interesting--

01:43:34.000 --> 01:43:35.750
I spent a lot of time
these past few years

01:43:35.750 --> 01:43:40.700
with industrial
ecologists who referred

01:43:40.700 --> 01:43:43.640
to the work of
natural ecologists.

01:43:43.640 --> 01:43:46.850
I'll leave it there in terms
of the whole ecology debate

01:43:46.850 --> 01:43:48.140
and is that a science or not.

01:43:48.140 --> 01:43:52.070
But there is a
very useful concept

01:43:52.070 --> 01:43:55.970
that I've used in
my teaching before.

01:43:55.970 --> 01:44:00.780
It's the concept of succession,
of the idea that there

01:44:00.780 --> 01:44:04.040
is a dynamic process by which
one ecosystem transforms

01:44:04.040 --> 01:44:07.940
into another over time and seems
to be done and seems to happen

01:44:07.940 --> 01:44:10.940
often in sort of step function.

01:44:10.940 --> 01:44:13.760
A rainforest will
transform into a savanna

01:44:13.760 --> 01:44:15.710
will transform into
a desert depending

01:44:15.710 --> 01:44:19.250
on a climactic changes
and other factors.

01:44:19.250 --> 01:44:21.410
One result of succession
is that the profile

01:44:21.410 --> 01:44:24.650
of resource consumption
for that location

01:44:24.650 --> 01:44:28.520
changes dramatically as it goes
from one kind of environment

01:44:28.520 --> 01:44:30.090
to another.

01:44:30.090 --> 01:44:32.290
We can actually
apply this to cities,

01:44:32.290 --> 01:44:38.640
especially rapidly urbanizing
urban centers around the world.

01:44:38.640 --> 01:44:40.590
And this is one
of my own graphs.

01:44:40.590 --> 01:44:45.930
This comes from research
on Chinese cities,

01:44:45.930 --> 01:44:48.480
a paper I wrote recently
published in the journal

01:44:48.480 --> 01:44:51.660
Industrial Ecology on a
massive urbanization of China.

01:44:51.660 --> 01:44:54.540
Basically, I think you can
break it down into three phases,

01:44:54.540 --> 01:44:57.180
especially as I said,
massively urbanizing, rapidly

01:44:57.180 --> 01:44:58.920
urbanizing cities.

01:44:58.920 --> 01:45:01.020
The first phase is
additions to stock,

01:45:01.020 --> 01:45:07.230
and that is an overwhelmingly
material intensive phase.

01:45:07.230 --> 01:45:09.340
The second is a
stabilization of that stock.

01:45:09.340 --> 01:45:11.520
So you're not building as much.

01:45:11.520 --> 01:45:15.110
It's becoming denser-- maybe
geographical limits of the city

01:45:15.110 --> 01:45:16.480
you're hitting--

01:45:16.480 --> 01:45:17.560
and it's tons of fun.

01:45:17.560 --> 01:45:18.780
So maybe not adding as much.

01:45:18.780 --> 01:45:20.590
You're adding quite a lot.

01:45:20.590 --> 01:45:28.830
And then the third phase is
the incremental densification.

01:45:28.830 --> 01:45:34.050
The third phase is actually
an energy intensive phase

01:45:34.050 --> 01:45:37.260
because once you've
added all the stock,

01:45:37.260 --> 01:45:39.390
the energy consumed
by that stock--

01:45:39.390 --> 01:45:40.950
stock, I mean buildings--

01:45:40.950 --> 01:45:42.720
increases dramatically.

01:45:42.720 --> 01:45:45.570
And as those buildings
live over time

01:45:45.570 --> 01:45:47.760
and they're renovated
and upgraded

01:45:47.760 --> 01:45:50.940
and more and more amenities
are inserted into them,

01:45:50.940 --> 01:45:53.370
the energy profile
of these cities

01:45:53.370 --> 01:45:58.470
changes so that this
curve, the material curve,

01:45:58.470 --> 01:45:59.850
goes up and then goes down.

01:45:59.850 --> 01:46:03.430
So, for example, this is
Shanghai few years ago.

01:46:03.430 --> 01:46:08.460
This is maybe
Manhattan or Chicago,

01:46:08.460 --> 01:46:09.785
and down here, this is Paris.

01:46:09.785 --> 01:46:11.160
You don't see a
lot of demolition

01:46:11.160 --> 01:46:12.298
in the center of Paris.

01:46:12.298 --> 01:46:13.840
You don't see a lot
of new buildings.

01:46:13.840 --> 01:46:16.920
So it's stabilized.

01:46:16.920 --> 01:46:21.030
What is important is
to notice that there's

01:46:21.030 --> 01:46:23.800
a change in the nature of
the resource consumption.

01:46:23.800 --> 01:46:27.840
And so right now, all
of these Asian cities

01:46:27.840 --> 01:46:31.180
that are growing
rapidly are in here.

01:46:31.180 --> 01:46:33.300
We have yet to see this
part of the curve--

01:46:33.300 --> 01:46:35.940
the big energy uptick.

01:46:35.940 --> 01:46:38.580
So that is what we're facing.

01:46:38.580 --> 01:46:41.010
Now consider the fact,
as I stated before,

01:46:41.010 --> 01:46:43.740
that 50% of the world--
more than 3 billion people--

01:46:43.740 --> 01:46:46.260
now live in cities, and the
world's population curve

01:46:46.260 --> 01:46:50.880
is headed for something like
9 to 10 billion, 20, 40, 50.

01:46:50.880 --> 01:46:52.890
If the global urban
population increases

01:46:52.890 --> 01:46:55.160
to a very conservative 60%--

01:46:55.160 --> 01:46:57.840
it will more likely be
somewhere in the 70s--

01:46:57.840 --> 01:46:59.520
at least 5 and
1/2 billion people

01:46:59.520 --> 01:47:02.130
will eventually be living
in these future cities.

01:47:02.130 --> 01:47:03.990
That's 2 and 1/2
billion more people

01:47:03.990 --> 01:47:06.300
in cities than today
consuming materials

01:47:06.300 --> 01:47:09.180
and energy at urban rates,
not at the rural rate

01:47:09.180 --> 01:47:12.870
that many of them are
now or would have been

01:47:12.870 --> 01:47:15.610
without massive urbanization.

01:47:15.610 --> 01:47:18.060
So this is what I mean by
the built environment--

01:47:18.060 --> 01:47:20.980
snapshot of today, a
little bit of the future.

01:47:20.980 --> 01:47:22.530
Let's go to question two.

01:47:22.530 --> 01:47:25.500
How much of our global
resources is consumed?

01:47:25.500 --> 01:47:29.750
I've touched a little bit
on a few consumption issues,

01:47:29.750 --> 01:47:32.260
but let me be a little
bit more specific.

01:47:32.260 --> 01:47:35.970
Let's start with the US.

01:47:35.970 --> 01:47:38.500
Let's start with the US
construction industry.

01:47:38.500 --> 01:47:43.650
Construction industry
is this vast messy thing

01:47:43.650 --> 01:47:48.618
of 220,000 separate businesses.

01:47:48.618 --> 01:47:50.160
The construction
industry is composed

01:47:50.160 --> 01:47:52.680
of that many
separate businesses.

01:47:52.680 --> 01:47:56.220
Most of them 1,
2, 3 person firms.

01:47:56.220 --> 01:47:59.820
That represents more than
$600 billion in annual revenue

01:47:59.820 --> 01:48:03.210
and employs roughly
2 million people.

01:48:03.210 --> 01:48:05.010
Notably, this
industry fragmented

01:48:05.010 --> 01:48:07.800
as it is into hundreds
of thousands of companies

01:48:07.800 --> 01:48:10.980
has historically lagged very far
behind other large industries

01:48:10.980 --> 01:48:14.010
in its investment for
research and development.

01:48:14.010 --> 01:48:16.140
You asked the question,
why are buildings

01:48:16.140 --> 01:48:20.250
built the same way today as
they were 50, 75 years ago?

01:48:20.250 --> 01:48:22.170
Well, the industry
spends about 1/10

01:48:22.170 --> 01:48:26.070
on research and development
as any other comparably sized

01:48:26.070 --> 01:48:28.110
or any other large
industry, really,

01:48:28.110 --> 01:48:31.460
even mid-sized industry.

01:48:31.460 --> 01:48:34.570
Let's go to the energy
consumption of that building

01:48:34.570 --> 01:48:36.940
stock in the US now.

01:48:36.940 --> 01:48:41.350
The built stock is produced
by this industry is really

01:48:41.350 --> 01:48:42.340
one of the--

01:48:42.340 --> 01:48:46.930
again, I mentioned that
not always mentioned

01:48:46.930 --> 01:48:50.040
consumer of primary energy.

01:48:50.040 --> 01:48:53.200
It does account for
40% of primary energy

01:48:53.200 --> 01:48:57.090
use residential and
commercial office buildings

01:48:57.090 --> 01:48:59.360
also 60% to 70%.

01:48:59.360 --> 01:49:02.070
And now I use that range
because now I'm talking

01:49:02.070 --> 01:49:03.450
about developed countries.

01:49:03.450 --> 01:49:07.350
It's fairly consistent
throughout electricity

01:49:07.350 --> 01:49:11.290
consumption, 60 of 70%.

01:49:11.290 --> 01:49:14.290
The figure of the
40% increases to 48%

01:49:14.290 --> 01:49:16.780
if one includes the energy
used in manufacture of building

01:49:16.780 --> 01:49:19.080
materials and construction.

01:49:19.080 --> 01:49:22.980
Operating buildings contributes
over 38% now closely correlated

01:49:22.980 --> 01:49:26.220
to electrical consumption
38% of carbon emissions

01:49:26.220 --> 01:49:29.190
of the United States, 38%.

01:49:29.190 --> 01:49:32.340
These numbers are consistent
across the developed world

01:49:32.340 --> 01:49:35.550
with some notable exceptions.

01:49:35.550 --> 01:49:37.530
Now in the developing
world, energy consumption

01:49:37.530 --> 01:49:40.110
devoted to the built environment
is a difficult number

01:49:40.110 --> 01:49:40.830
to get at--

01:49:40.830 --> 01:49:43.180
hard to get good
data and though there

01:49:43.180 --> 01:49:45.310
are a number of
studies to be done.

01:49:45.310 --> 01:49:46.710
But we can note
a couple of facts

01:49:46.710 --> 01:49:49.470
that outline the future
of energy consumption

01:49:49.470 --> 01:49:50.760
in the next couple of decades.

01:49:50.760 --> 01:49:54.450
And using the OECD
country designations,

01:49:54.450 --> 01:49:59.040
in 2015, non-OECD countries
will surpass the OECD

01:49:59.040 --> 01:50:05.920
group in their overall energy
consumption, 2015, eight years.

01:50:05.920 --> 01:50:07.600
Between now and 2030.

01:50:07.600 --> 01:50:10.930
Non-OECD countries will
account for 3/4 of the increase

01:50:10.930 --> 01:50:12.680
in energy consumption.

01:50:12.680 --> 01:50:14.850
That's Asia, Africa, others.

01:50:14.850 --> 01:50:16.600
And by that year, their
energy consumption

01:50:16.600 --> 01:50:22.000
will exceed that
of the OECD by 34%.

01:50:22.000 --> 01:50:24.910
Let's take one
component, only one.

01:50:24.910 --> 01:50:27.910
I don't have time to go through
heating and cooling and all

01:50:27.910 --> 01:50:30.250
the other, but let's
just take one--

01:50:30.250 --> 01:50:32.320
again, one that's
not often mentioned

01:50:32.320 --> 01:50:33.820
and that is the
energy and lighting.

01:50:33.820 --> 01:50:38.500
19% of global electricity use
is devoted to lighting alone--

01:50:38.500 --> 01:50:40.860
almost 20%.

01:50:40.860 --> 01:50:43.690
I love to show this image with
this sort of sobering fact

01:50:43.690 --> 01:50:44.190
before it.

01:50:44.190 --> 01:50:47.340
Believe me, it gets more
positive a little later.

01:50:47.340 --> 01:50:50.640
I love to show this image below.

01:50:50.640 --> 01:50:52.260
I mean, above that
fact because this

01:50:52.260 --> 01:50:56.760
is a design of Santiago
Calatrava in Milwaukee Art

01:50:56.760 --> 01:51:01.170
Museum, and it's a wholly daylit
space as part of the main hall.

01:51:01.170 --> 01:51:03.300
In fact, photograph
of MIT undergraduate

01:51:03.300 --> 01:51:07.040
David Fox, who just graduated
a couple of years ago.

01:51:07.040 --> 01:51:11.940
Okay, let's also talk about
construction materials.

01:51:11.940 --> 01:51:13.800
There's huge material
flows associated

01:51:13.800 --> 01:51:15.220
with the built environment.

01:51:15.220 --> 01:51:18.390
In fact, a study showed that
70% by weight of all material

01:51:18.390 --> 01:51:20.730
flows through most developed
industrialized countries

01:51:20.730 --> 01:51:22.350
is devoted to construction.

01:51:22.350 --> 01:51:24.900
20% to 30% of the entire
societal waste stream

01:51:24.900 --> 01:51:28.830
stems from construction and
demolition waste or nearly 60%

01:51:28.830 --> 01:51:30.330
of total nonindustrial waste.

01:51:33.360 --> 01:51:36.120
Of this construction and
demolition waste stream,

01:51:36.120 --> 01:51:42.300
48% is a result of demolition,
44% result of renovation,

01:51:42.300 --> 01:51:44.550
with the remainder
being new construction.

01:51:44.550 --> 01:51:47.100
Today, in the States,
we're recovering

01:51:47.100 --> 01:51:48.730
approximately 20% to 30%--

01:51:48.730 --> 01:51:50.640
depending on the year--

01:51:50.640 --> 01:51:53.730
of construction and
demolition waste.

01:51:53.730 --> 01:51:56.110
I know you can't see the thing.

01:51:56.110 --> 01:51:59.490
That's a million tons.

01:51:59.490 --> 01:52:01.290
This is year.

01:52:01.290 --> 01:52:04.230
This is construction.

01:52:04.230 --> 01:52:06.180
I can't even read it myself.

01:52:06.180 --> 01:52:10.080
Industrial and is that
agriculture is at the bottom?

01:52:10.080 --> 01:52:13.800
Yeah, okay, from
what I remember.

01:52:13.800 --> 01:52:16.800
I mean, you can also look at a
per capita material consumption

01:52:16.800 --> 01:52:22.450
in the United States, and these
are kind of amusing graphic

01:52:22.450 --> 01:52:22.950
here.

01:52:22.950 --> 01:52:27.090
3.6 million pounds of
minerals, metals and fuels

01:52:27.090 --> 01:52:29.940
are consumed by every
American in their lifetime.

01:52:29.940 --> 01:52:32.640
A huge bulk of that
is construction.

01:52:32.640 --> 01:52:35.460
The movement on all that
material costs a lot of energy.

01:52:35.460 --> 01:52:37.860
Let's talk just a little
bit more about materials

01:52:37.860 --> 01:52:40.320
because materials is
kind of my thing also--

01:52:40.320 --> 01:52:45.870
spent a good few years studying
the materials of construction.

01:52:48.790 --> 01:52:51.790
Looking at the material flows
for construction and material

01:52:51.790 --> 01:52:54.610
flows in society generally has
led to some landmark studies

01:52:54.610 --> 01:52:55.360
recently.

01:52:55.360 --> 01:53:00.540
Colleague of mine--
well, at Yale--

01:53:00.540 --> 01:53:02.575
Tom [INAUDIBLE],,
he's in the school

01:53:02.575 --> 01:53:05.200
forestry and the environment has
done a whole series of studies

01:53:05.200 --> 01:53:07.390
on the material
flow of the existing

01:53:07.390 --> 01:53:11.800
stocks of primary metals, and
he has shown rather conclusively

01:53:11.800 --> 01:53:15.610
that a third of that is
left in the ground in very

01:53:15.610 --> 01:53:16.930
low-grade ores.

01:53:16.930 --> 01:53:20.840
A third is in circulation
in buildings like this

01:53:20.840 --> 01:53:23.620
and automobiles,
industrial equipment.

01:53:23.620 --> 01:53:25.960
And a third has
been lost forever

01:53:25.960 --> 01:53:30.500
in that process dissipated
to the environment.

01:53:30.500 --> 01:53:32.830
Why is this important
or useful to know?

01:53:32.830 --> 01:53:36.340
Well, we're slowly approaching
not limits to growth,

01:53:36.340 --> 01:53:40.390
but actually, the
realization that some

01:53:40.390 --> 01:53:45.695
of the best locations, some of
the richest locations for some

01:53:45.695 --> 01:53:47.320
of the more important
materials we have

01:53:47.320 --> 01:53:48.850
are not in this kind of place.

01:53:48.850 --> 01:53:50.570
This is a copper mine.

01:53:50.570 --> 01:53:54.100
It's just that the mine
tailings that you see

01:53:54.100 --> 01:53:56.410
from the air, an aerial photo.

01:53:56.410 --> 01:54:00.010
But actually, copper
concentrations

01:54:00.010 --> 01:54:04.060
are now starting to
be richest in cities.

01:54:04.060 --> 01:54:06.400
This is a graph of
actually Beijing.

01:54:06.400 --> 01:54:10.840
And each one of these bars is
not a proposed super skyscraper

01:54:10.840 --> 01:54:14.140
but the concentration
of copper in that city.

01:54:14.140 --> 01:54:20.140
Let's remember a very
prescient, poignant, in fact,

01:54:20.140 --> 01:54:22.240
quote of Jane
Jacobs, "our cities

01:54:22.240 --> 01:54:24.560
are the minds of the future."

01:54:24.560 --> 01:54:26.980
Let me tell you my own
personal experience,

01:54:26.980 --> 01:54:29.930
not only in New
Orleans specifically,

01:54:29.930 --> 01:54:31.682
but in other cities.

01:54:31.682 --> 01:54:33.640
There's a funny thing
that's happened recently.

01:54:33.640 --> 01:54:35.380
There's a recent
emergence of what's

01:54:35.380 --> 01:54:41.262
called midnight plumbers,
and these are in primarily--

01:54:41.262 --> 01:54:43.720
they're starting to show up in
inner city housing complexes

01:54:43.720 --> 01:54:48.760
mostly from Baltimore
to New Orleans.

01:54:48.760 --> 01:54:50.860
And what they are,
as you can imagine,

01:54:50.860 --> 01:54:53.980
is they are people who
are stripping the plumbing

01:54:53.980 --> 01:54:59.740
from unoccupied or lightly
occupied or temporarily vacant

01:54:59.740 --> 01:55:01.510
apartments.

01:55:01.510 --> 01:55:05.200
And I read an article recently
the New York Times, which

01:55:05.200 --> 01:55:06.720
has had this interesting fact.

01:55:06.720 --> 01:55:11.170
The landlord had said that he
had noticed this was happening,

01:55:11.170 --> 01:55:13.050
and he had a specific number.

01:55:13.050 --> 01:55:15.150
He said, when 20
pounds of copper

01:55:15.150 --> 01:55:18.895
equals or exceeds the
minimum wage for one day,

01:55:18.895 --> 01:55:20.520
you start seeing this
stuff showing up.

01:55:24.885 --> 01:55:26.760
I don't want to talk
about limits of growth--

01:55:26.760 --> 01:55:28.593
although you can ask
me questions about it--

01:55:28.593 --> 01:55:31.590
but I do want to talk about
the shifting of the location

01:55:31.590 --> 01:55:35.400
and the accessibility
to important materials

01:55:35.400 --> 01:55:39.721
for society, but also for the
built environment, generally.

01:55:42.430 --> 01:55:46.780
Now this brings up an
issue about the way

01:55:46.780 --> 01:55:50.710
we treat our buildings
because it is very clear

01:55:50.710 --> 01:55:55.180
that until very recently,
the only measure of value

01:55:55.180 --> 01:55:57.475
for our buildings has
been a financial measure.

01:55:57.475 --> 01:55:59.890
And it's a real
estate instrument.

01:55:59.890 --> 01:56:03.910
It is on a ledger as
an abstract thing.

01:56:03.910 --> 01:56:08.380
It is not necessarily
considered a physical thing.

01:56:08.380 --> 01:56:10.750
I wish you could hear all
the cheers of the crowd

01:56:10.750 --> 01:56:13.230
here coming out of my laptop.

01:56:13.230 --> 01:56:17.500
But these buildings
have been treated

01:56:17.500 --> 01:56:21.130
as essentially things that can
be moved around the chessboard.

01:56:21.130 --> 01:56:25.660
Well, I think we're
hitting the time when

01:56:25.660 --> 01:56:29.533
the up and coming professionals
designers, architects,

01:56:29.533 --> 01:56:31.450
engineers, are starting
to see that that's not

01:56:31.450 --> 01:56:33.580
such an easy game to play
any longer, especially

01:56:33.580 --> 01:56:36.620
with our infrastructure.

01:56:36.620 --> 01:56:39.190
This was, by the way, was
the demolition of the Seattle

01:56:39.190 --> 01:56:41.920
kingdom, and it was
an amazing building.

01:56:41.920 --> 01:56:44.230
It was actually the
longest thin shell concrete

01:56:44.230 --> 01:56:45.920
span in the world.

01:56:45.920 --> 01:56:51.610
Its cousin, the pantheon,
has lasted for a long time.

01:56:51.610 --> 01:56:55.890
The Seattle kingdom
lasted 25 years.

01:56:55.890 --> 01:56:58.360
When it was only
25 years almost,

01:56:58.360 --> 01:56:59.770
demolished just out of college.

01:57:07.330 --> 01:57:09.582
And when you read
about green buildings,

01:57:09.582 --> 01:57:11.290
one of the first things
you'll read about

01:57:11.290 --> 01:57:13.960
is green materials,
recycled materials,

01:57:13.960 --> 01:57:14.890
low-energy materials.

01:57:14.890 --> 01:57:16.960
Well, let's be real.

01:57:16.960 --> 01:57:21.830
Actually, in terms of societal
consumption of materials,

01:57:21.830 --> 01:57:23.350
it's pretty clear
we're not being

01:57:23.350 --> 01:57:27.110
any more renewable
than we used to be--

01:57:27.110 --> 01:57:30.850
so say, 1950 proportion.

01:57:30.850 --> 01:57:35.110
So this is renewable
versus non-renewable

01:57:35.110 --> 01:57:37.600
versus renewable
materials, proportions

01:57:37.600 --> 01:57:39.940
of which societal consumption.

01:57:39.940 --> 01:57:43.040
So this includes other things
besides the built environment

01:57:43.040 --> 01:57:45.640
but as we know, 70% by
weight material flow.

01:57:45.640 --> 01:57:49.390
So the built environment
plays a large role here.

01:57:49.390 --> 01:57:52.450
That's my disclaimer.

01:57:52.450 --> 01:57:54.610
In 1950, we were there.

01:57:54.610 --> 01:57:56.620
Today, we're not
doing any better.

01:57:56.620 --> 01:58:00.580
We're doing worse 95%
non-renewable to 5%.

01:58:00.580 --> 01:58:02.560
So this is a trend
that we really

01:58:02.560 --> 01:58:06.550
do have to think about,
especially when we factor

01:58:06.550 --> 01:58:10.360
in the energy that it takes
to push all of these materials

01:58:10.360 --> 01:58:12.060
around.

01:58:12.060 --> 01:58:14.982
Okay, I want to round
out question two.

01:58:14.982 --> 01:58:15.940
How am I doing on time?

01:58:15.940 --> 01:58:17.010
I got 33 minutes.

01:58:20.090 --> 01:58:25.630
With a composite measure that,
I'm sure you are all very fond.

01:58:25.630 --> 01:58:28.820
I'm sure some of you are better
informed about this than I am.

01:58:28.820 --> 01:58:31.700
But the ecological footprint--

01:58:31.700 --> 01:58:34.430
a composite measure
developed the latest version

01:58:34.430 --> 01:58:38.940
developed by [INAUDIBLE]
They keep a website

01:58:38.940 --> 01:58:42.590
at globalfootprintnetwork.org.

01:58:42.590 --> 01:58:45.920
The ecological
footprint is a measure

01:58:45.920 --> 01:58:48.350
of the global societal
demands versus supply

01:58:48.350 --> 01:58:50.480
of biologically productive
land for everything

01:58:50.480 --> 01:58:53.090
that we do from
agriculture to construction

01:58:53.090 --> 01:58:55.850
to manufacturer
building materials.

01:58:55.850 --> 01:58:59.930
A couple of recent or
relatively recent thresholds--

01:58:59.930 --> 01:59:02.910
one particularly ominous
one that we crossed--

01:59:02.910 --> 01:59:05.090
and you can certainly
debate the way

01:59:05.090 --> 01:59:09.560
they calculate the ecological
biocapacity of the earth.

01:59:09.560 --> 01:59:13.880
But in the late
1980s, we crossed

01:59:13.880 --> 01:59:17.920
this is number of planet Earths.

01:59:17.920 --> 01:59:24.770
So the biocapacity of one planet
Earth is outlined right there.

01:59:24.770 --> 01:59:27.540
This is years, 60, 70, 80, 90.

01:59:31.180 --> 01:59:33.753
and this is a huge physical
accounting exercise

01:59:33.753 --> 01:59:35.420
that the group goes
through, and they've

01:59:35.420 --> 01:59:39.410
been doing for its
peer-reviewed massive effort.

01:59:39.410 --> 01:59:41.150
In the late '80s,
they determined

01:59:41.150 --> 01:59:43.610
that we crossed the
threshold in going

01:59:43.610 --> 01:59:46.820
from on an annual
basis demanding more

01:59:46.820 --> 01:59:48.810
than the supply could give us.

01:59:48.810 --> 01:59:52.770
That doesn't mean that
you can't supply all

01:59:52.770 --> 01:59:54.430
of that demand in the future.

01:59:54.430 --> 01:59:56.550
It just means that,
well, we're digging

01:59:56.550 --> 01:59:59.775
into the natural
capital, basically.

01:59:59.775 --> 02:00:03.180
And you dig into natural
capital far enough,

02:00:03.180 --> 02:00:05.940
and you can endanger
certain ecologies.

02:00:05.940 --> 02:00:09.900
Like an old-growth
forest, you log it enough,

02:00:09.900 --> 02:00:13.230
and it's no longer a
biodiverse environment

02:00:13.230 --> 02:00:15.030
it's something else.

02:00:15.030 --> 02:00:19.530
In 1960, the population of
the world was 3 billion,

02:00:19.530 --> 02:00:23.800
2,006.1, actually.

02:00:23.800 --> 02:00:28.190
Interesting thing is, while
you double it and you get more.

02:00:28.190 --> 02:00:33.580
Of this the ecological
footprint was 0.5 Earth's.

02:00:33.580 --> 02:00:35.180
That's 1.2.

02:00:35.180 --> 02:00:36.740
And the difference
between those two,

02:00:36.740 --> 02:00:39.640
if one would expect that
maybe a 0.5 at six billion

02:00:39.640 --> 02:00:40.575
would be one.

02:00:40.575 --> 02:00:41.950
Well, the difference
between them

02:00:41.950 --> 02:00:43.870
is increased the
increase in affluence

02:00:43.870 --> 02:00:47.110
and our increasing ability to
extract the resources that we

02:00:47.110 --> 02:00:50.560
want so that right now,
we're at 1.2 Earths

02:00:50.560 --> 02:00:53.110
and scheduled to
continue rising.

02:00:53.110 --> 02:00:57.880
This feeds into the discussions
about tipping points

02:00:57.880 --> 02:00:59.770
on various ecologies
around the world.

02:01:02.350 --> 02:01:07.390
Okay, so that's the
built environment

02:01:07.390 --> 02:01:12.610
the filtered way that I see
it for today in 45 minutes.

02:01:12.610 --> 02:01:14.650
The third question,
let's address that.

02:01:18.610 --> 02:01:22.150
Should we change the future?

02:01:22.150 --> 02:01:23.890
Well, I'm already
convinced because I'm

02:01:23.890 --> 02:01:27.550
one of four points on a
slide and Professor Deutsch's

02:01:27.550 --> 02:01:29.500
presentation that,
yeah, we should

02:01:29.500 --> 02:01:33.100
because we're talking
about moderating demand,

02:01:33.100 --> 02:01:35.890
essentially and designing
better so that demand

02:01:35.890 --> 02:01:38.150
doesn't increase.

02:01:38.150 --> 02:01:47.260
There's a lot I could say about
using the ecological footprint

02:01:47.260 --> 02:01:49.450
to answer this question.

02:01:49.450 --> 02:01:53.680
Let me suggest a
direct answer for now.

02:01:53.680 --> 02:01:57.153
Today and tomorrow
and into the future,

02:01:57.153 --> 02:01:58.570
this is something
that you can do.

02:01:58.570 --> 02:01:59.945
Look at the
buildings around you,

02:01:59.945 --> 02:02:02.380
including this one
buildings that you live

02:02:02.380 --> 02:02:05.310
and work in the
buildings that you visit.

02:02:05.310 --> 02:02:09.530
For most of those buildings
in the United States,

02:02:09.530 --> 02:02:12.830
you can decrease its energy
consumption by 10% to 15%

02:02:12.830 --> 02:02:15.380
with a buyback time
of about five years--

02:02:15.380 --> 02:02:16.590
no question about it.

02:02:16.590 --> 02:02:18.360
You can do it tomorrow.

02:02:18.360 --> 02:02:19.730
Those are efficiency savings--

02:02:19.730 --> 02:02:22.250
better insulation
and better windows.

02:02:22.250 --> 02:02:23.930
The way fuel prices are going--

02:02:23.930 --> 02:02:27.000
heating, fuel prices
and electricity--

02:02:27.000 --> 02:02:30.440
it is possible to do that
within a five-year payback.

02:02:30.440 --> 02:02:33.380
This can best be done--

02:02:33.380 --> 02:02:38.470
I'm also not going to talk about
solar because this can best be

02:02:38.470 --> 02:02:41.500
done with an
integrated approach,

02:02:41.500 --> 02:02:45.490
accessing renewable energy
sources-- solar, micro,

02:02:45.490 --> 02:02:50.770
wind turbines and
other technologies--

02:02:50.770 --> 02:02:54.870
But it should be coupled with
efficiency, conserving energy,

02:02:54.870 --> 02:02:56.970
be more efficient in our
use of the energy that's

02:02:56.970 --> 02:03:00.237
already provided to
us for our buildings.

02:03:00.237 --> 02:03:02.820
Let me give you another answer
to this question, which I think

02:03:02.820 --> 02:03:09.130
should be a kind of a rule
of thumb for green building

02:03:09.130 --> 02:03:10.810
people around the world.

02:03:10.810 --> 02:03:15.460
And that is it's probably
best to increase the energy

02:03:15.460 --> 02:03:18.668
efficiency in existing
buildings in the developed world

02:03:18.668 --> 02:03:20.710
because we have a lot of
stock, and we have a lot

02:03:20.710 --> 02:03:22.270
of very underperforming stock.

02:03:22.270 --> 02:03:25.520
Still build a lot but we
certainly don't build this

02:03:25.520 --> 02:03:28.722
as much or will build as
much as the developing world.

02:03:28.722 --> 02:03:30.430
And that leads to the
second point, which

02:03:30.430 --> 02:03:33.280
is it's very
important to increase

02:03:33.280 --> 02:03:34.990
the efficiencies in
the way new buildings

02:03:34.990 --> 02:03:38.260
are brought on online
in developing countries.

02:03:38.260 --> 02:03:39.760
Couple those two
together and you've

02:03:39.760 --> 02:03:43.180
got a very powerful strategy
for moderating demand globally

02:03:43.180 --> 02:03:44.860
for the built environment.

02:03:44.860 --> 02:03:45.800
You know what?

02:03:45.800 --> 02:03:47.710
I spend a lot of
time with students.

02:03:47.710 --> 02:03:48.750
I teach a lot.

02:03:48.750 --> 02:03:52.710
I just got tenured
thanks to class of '57,

02:03:52.710 --> 02:03:55.096
and I want to say you
guys really helped out.

02:03:55.096 --> 02:04:00.940
[APPLAUSE]

02:04:00.940 --> 02:04:04.110
So actually, what I want to
bring in here are students.

02:04:04.110 --> 02:04:06.610
I want to answer the question
from the student point of view

02:04:06.610 --> 02:04:08.920
because I see the student
body has been extraordinarily

02:04:08.920 --> 02:04:11.200
powerful force in all of this.

02:04:11.200 --> 02:04:14.230
I want to answer the question
through Tom Weathers.

02:04:14.230 --> 02:04:16.810
Tom, if you're watching, hello.

02:04:16.810 --> 02:04:20.170
Tom is a recently
graduated just yesterday

02:04:20.170 --> 02:04:23.500
master of architecture
student MIT,

02:04:23.500 --> 02:04:26.350
and he dedicated his thesis--

02:04:26.350 --> 02:04:31.660
full three semesters of
prethesis research and then

02:04:31.660 --> 02:04:35.410
actually writing it to
investigating the possibilities

02:04:35.410 --> 02:04:38.590
for using the existing material
flow here on campus, the MIT

02:04:38.590 --> 02:04:40.450
campus, particularly
underutilized

02:04:40.450 --> 02:04:42.820
the valuable waste
stream materials

02:04:42.820 --> 02:04:45.190
in the design and construction
of a low-energy building.

02:04:45.190 --> 02:04:48.130
His question was, is there
enough flow around us

02:04:48.130 --> 02:04:51.790
that we can maybe capture some
of it and make a good building?

02:04:51.790 --> 02:04:54.610
He produced as a
result of this study

02:04:54.610 --> 02:04:57.130
as far as I know, the first
comprehensive material flow

02:04:57.130 --> 02:04:59.172
analysis of the MIT community.

02:04:59.172 --> 02:05:00.880
And then he analyzed
it for the potential

02:05:00.880 --> 02:05:04.420
for using new buildings.

02:05:04.420 --> 02:05:06.970
So the idea is so waste
that's floating out there,

02:05:06.970 --> 02:05:08.740
capture it and make
a new building--

02:05:08.740 --> 02:05:14.940
sounds very academic in some
ways kind of unseemly, I guess.

02:05:14.940 --> 02:05:19.090
But in Lexington,
just north of here,

02:05:19.090 --> 02:05:23.680
there is a house that was built
of the detritus-- previously

02:05:23.680 --> 02:05:28.750
the detritus [INAUDIBLE] most
of the primary structural

02:05:28.750 --> 02:05:32.380
elements, many of the very
high embodied energy materials

02:05:32.380 --> 02:05:33.940
in this building.

02:05:33.940 --> 02:05:35.950
A lot of the exterior
envelope materials

02:05:35.950 --> 02:05:37.603
were directly captured
from that flow.

02:05:37.603 --> 02:05:39.520
And you can look at the
big dig and say, well,

02:05:39.520 --> 02:05:42.830
that was a huge material flow.

02:05:42.830 --> 02:05:46.750
Well, some of that was
captured a tiny, tiny little

02:05:46.750 --> 02:05:49.220
less than a drop was
captured for this building,

02:05:49.220 --> 02:05:51.130
but it's a really
interesting approach

02:05:51.130 --> 02:05:53.170
to the way a
designer would think

02:05:53.170 --> 02:05:57.610
about what are the resources
that I have at my disposal.

02:05:57.610 --> 02:06:00.460
Another student I
want to mention here

02:06:00.460 --> 02:06:03.850
is Lucille [INAUDIBLE].

02:06:03.850 --> 02:06:06.868
Lucille is a current master
of architecture student.

02:06:06.868 --> 02:06:08.660
They both gave me
permission to show these.

02:06:08.660 --> 02:06:10.600
They're happy with it.

02:06:10.600 --> 02:06:14.320
And she's also
very much a person

02:06:14.320 --> 02:06:17.500
who thinks that we
should change the future.

02:06:17.500 --> 02:06:22.690
Her project of last term
in the design studio

02:06:22.690 --> 02:06:26.440
brought into focus the need to
consider architectural form as

02:06:26.440 --> 02:06:30.340
shaped by the potential offered
by solar radiation by the sun--

02:06:30.340 --> 02:06:32.620
an old topic.

02:06:32.620 --> 02:06:34.660
But so much of our
architectural form

02:06:34.660 --> 02:06:36.580
has nothing to do with the sun.

02:06:36.580 --> 02:06:39.250
So much of our architectural
form is oriented to what?

02:06:39.250 --> 02:06:42.588
The street-- fly
anywhere to look

02:06:42.588 --> 02:06:44.380
at the little curving
streets and where how

02:06:44.380 --> 02:06:45.770
are the houses looking.

02:06:45.770 --> 02:06:47.060
They follow the streets.

02:06:47.060 --> 02:06:51.250
So her idea is that there
needs to be design, technology,

02:06:51.250 --> 02:06:53.710
and planning that
directly proposes

02:06:53.710 --> 02:06:55.420
that the configuration
of buildings

02:06:55.420 --> 02:06:58.130
should follow potential
for solar heat

02:06:58.130 --> 02:07:00.100
gains, solar shading,
not a new idea.

02:07:00.100 --> 02:07:03.130
In fact, let's remember
that this approach really

02:07:03.130 --> 02:07:05.090
had its origins--

02:07:05.090 --> 02:07:06.010
one of its origins--

02:07:06.010 --> 02:07:09.460
here in the solar houses
at MIT in the 1930s--

02:07:09.460 --> 02:07:11.050
way back in the 1930s.

02:07:11.050 --> 02:07:14.860
In fact, in 1938, the Committee
on Solar Energy Utilization

02:07:14.860 --> 02:07:16.620
at MIT was set up.

02:07:16.620 --> 02:07:21.240
And for the next 50
years, until 1988,

02:07:21.240 --> 02:07:23.980
a series of six experimental
and prototypical houses

02:07:23.980 --> 02:07:28.060
were built here at MIT and
served very much as the seeds

02:07:28.060 --> 02:07:29.710
to things like this.

02:07:29.710 --> 02:07:32.195
This is the Passive
House Institute.

02:07:32.195 --> 02:07:33.820
This is material from
the Passive House

02:07:33.820 --> 02:07:34.810
Institute in Germany.

02:07:34.810 --> 02:07:38.110
A person there named
Wolfgang Feist paid attention

02:07:38.110 --> 02:07:40.360
to the MIT work,
and he spent a lot

02:07:40.360 --> 02:07:45.400
of time perfecting a combination
of super insulated shell

02:07:45.400 --> 02:07:47.110
with heat recovery.

02:07:47.110 --> 02:07:50.380
The breath that I breathe,
the heat that I'm producing

02:07:50.380 --> 02:07:54.010
can be recovered and
then preheat incoming air

02:07:54.010 --> 02:07:57.070
from the outside,
high performance

02:07:57.070 --> 02:08:00.303
windows and a systematic
elimination of thermal bridges

02:08:00.303 --> 02:08:01.720
between the inside
and the outside

02:08:01.720 --> 02:08:04.780
the cold outside in the
warm inside, for example.

02:08:04.780 --> 02:08:08.560
And he produced a
design, a passive house,

02:08:08.560 --> 02:08:11.440
and it's a really
important approach

02:08:11.440 --> 02:08:14.080
to residential architecture.

02:08:14.080 --> 02:08:17.410
For the United States, 68%
percent of our US housing stock

02:08:17.410 --> 02:08:21.820
is still single family
housing, and the median age

02:08:21.820 --> 02:08:25.100
of our US housing
stock is 27 years,

02:08:25.100 --> 02:08:27.460
which means that we're a
country of new housing.

02:08:27.460 --> 02:08:28.990
And this kind of
approach still has

02:08:28.990 --> 02:08:32.020
relevance-- we can still apply.

02:08:32.020 --> 02:08:33.950
10,000 of these houses
have been built.

02:08:33.950 --> 02:08:35.000
This is not a pipe dream.

02:08:35.000 --> 02:08:37.100
This is not the Wolfgang
[INAUDIBLE] one office.

02:08:37.100 --> 02:08:39.100
Okay, so these MIT people
have done all of this.

02:08:39.100 --> 02:08:40.600
Now I want to make it real.

02:08:40.600 --> 02:08:42.260
I want to make it
happen in the world.

02:08:42.260 --> 02:08:42.890
And he did--

02:08:42.890 --> 02:08:45.320
10,000 of them built in Germany.

02:08:45.320 --> 02:08:48.280
The Passive Institute
has expanded its range

02:08:48.280 --> 02:08:51.430
to include other building
types, multifamily, multiunit

02:08:51.430 --> 02:08:54.190
residential buildings, apartment
houses, schools, office

02:08:54.190 --> 02:08:56.370
buildings, community centers.

02:08:56.370 --> 02:09:01.340
The passive house-- let
me be very direct clear.

02:09:01.340 --> 02:09:03.300
There's been a whole
series of conferences

02:09:03.300 --> 02:09:04.800
on the passive
house-- lots and lots

02:09:04.800 --> 02:09:06.437
of data taken over 10 years.

02:09:06.437 --> 02:09:08.770
I think there have been seven
passive house conferences.

02:09:08.770 --> 02:09:11.520
I know that probably
wrong, but it's close.

02:09:11.520 --> 02:09:12.720
This is typical.

02:09:17.880 --> 02:09:24.120
So the kilowatt hour
per meter squared here--

02:09:24.120 --> 02:09:28.990
a passive house-- welded
built passive house--

02:09:28.990 --> 02:09:35.380
but perfectly well verified
and standard passive house

02:09:35.380 --> 02:09:40.890
construction will lead to an
85% to 90% reduction in energy.

02:09:40.890 --> 02:09:48.413
This is in Germany
and northern Europe.

02:09:48.413 --> 02:09:49.830
There is a group
that I'm actually

02:09:49.830 --> 02:09:51.960
talking to quite a lot
who's got a demonstration

02:09:51.960 --> 02:09:53.330
project in Uxbridge.

02:09:53.330 --> 02:09:55.080
They're bringing they've
actually licensed

02:09:55.080 --> 02:09:56.567
the passive House technology.

02:09:56.567 --> 02:09:57.900
They're bringing it to Oxbridge.

02:09:57.900 --> 02:10:00.040
They're going to build
a building there.

02:10:00.040 --> 02:10:02.040
MIT hopefully we'll
be monitoring it

02:10:02.040 --> 02:10:07.050
so that the passive house
can become commercialized

02:10:07.050 --> 02:10:10.370
in this country.

02:10:10.370 --> 02:10:17.860
So in my opinion and in
light of the first kind

02:10:17.860 --> 02:10:20.590
of sobering 2/3 of
this presentation,

02:10:20.590 --> 02:10:22.180
I think all of this
last few slides

02:10:22.180 --> 02:10:26.340
is very good news because the
built environment does offer

02:10:26.340 --> 02:10:30.840
an opportunity to
improve our resource

02:10:30.840 --> 02:10:33.250
efficiency while
revisiting again,

02:10:33.250 --> 02:10:35.250
the value that we place
on architectural spaces

02:10:35.250 --> 02:10:36.948
in the public realm.

02:10:36.948 --> 02:10:38.490
This is actually
one of my buildings.

02:10:38.490 --> 02:10:40.380
I had to throw it up there.

02:10:40.380 --> 02:10:43.650
It is actually before I had any
of these thoughts in my mind,

02:10:43.650 --> 02:10:44.910
before I came to MIT.

02:10:44.910 --> 02:10:47.000
This is actually in
addition to the law school

02:10:47.000 --> 02:10:48.330
at Columbia University.

02:10:48.330 --> 02:10:50.910
They needed public space,
and it's south-oriented.

02:10:50.910 --> 02:10:53.790
And these aluminum fins and
that [INAUDIBLE] glass, which

02:10:53.790 --> 02:10:55.910
is a kind of veiled glass.

02:10:55.910 --> 02:10:59.520
So the enamel baked
onto the glass

02:10:59.520 --> 02:11:05.010
is basically designed for
solar heat in winter and none

02:11:05.010 --> 02:11:07.080
in the summer-- very,
very straightforward idea.

02:11:09.730 --> 02:11:14.670
Another kind of project which
really, my project showed

02:11:14.670 --> 02:11:16.830
before really did
come out of the work

02:11:16.830 --> 02:11:20.700
of the MIT solar houses and
idea of solar architecture.

02:11:20.700 --> 02:11:23.350
Other projects have come out
of that, other technologies,

02:11:23.350 --> 02:11:26.850
including aerogels, super
insulating silica foam

02:11:26.850 --> 02:11:30.360
materials having
upwards of 10 to 20

02:11:30.360 --> 02:11:34.950
times the thermal resistance of
typical insulating materials.

02:11:34.950 --> 02:11:37.050
And another building,
okay, so this

02:11:37.050 --> 02:11:40.830
is solar house taken to an
extreme commercial office

02:11:40.830 --> 02:11:41.760
building.

02:11:41.760 --> 02:11:44.190
We see them all over the
place, glass buildings.

02:11:44.190 --> 02:11:45.570
This building shades itself.

02:11:45.570 --> 02:11:46.350
It shields itself.

02:11:46.350 --> 02:11:48.183
It's very straightforward,
very simple idea.

02:11:48.183 --> 02:11:50.400
We don't see a lot
of this around so

02:11:50.400 --> 02:11:51.580
that's why I'm an optimist.

02:11:51.580 --> 02:11:53.520
I think there are very
straightforward, simple

02:11:53.520 --> 02:11:58.110
actually quite beautiful
solutions to moderating demand.

02:12:01.030 --> 02:12:04.030
We do need material research,
fundamental material research.

02:12:04.030 --> 02:12:07.990
And I've done some assemblies
research, depending

02:12:07.990 --> 02:12:09.168
on the materials scientists.

02:12:09.168 --> 02:12:10.960
We do need inexpensive
and high performance

02:12:10.960 --> 02:12:12.168
thermal insulation materials.

02:12:12.168 --> 02:12:14.780
We still do, inexpensive.

02:12:14.780 --> 02:12:17.540
Responsive, spectrally
selective coatings for glass

02:12:17.540 --> 02:12:20.180
desiccate materials of all
kinds to moderate humidity

02:12:20.180 --> 02:12:21.950
when you bring in outside air.

02:12:21.950 --> 02:12:25.190
Self-healing vapor
and air barriers--

02:12:25.190 --> 02:12:28.640
I think we're on the cusp
of a number of breakthroughs

02:12:28.640 --> 02:12:30.210
there and more.

02:12:30.210 --> 02:12:31.660
We need more
fundamental research

02:12:31.660 --> 02:12:33.140
on the materials for buildings.

02:12:33.140 --> 02:12:34.390
And we're seeing some of this.

02:12:34.390 --> 02:12:39.650
We're seeing the more
inventive, maybe not as

02:12:39.650 --> 02:12:41.990
risk averse, or maybe very
successful architects.

02:12:41.990 --> 02:12:44.540
So they can take some
risks doing these things

02:12:44.540 --> 02:12:47.005
on a big scale.

02:12:47.005 --> 02:12:49.130
These are shots from Lorenzo
piano's New York Times

02:12:49.130 --> 02:12:50.120
building in Times Square.

02:12:50.120 --> 02:12:52.430
If you haven't seen it here
in New York City, go visit.

02:12:52.430 --> 02:12:55.170
It's a building with a
huge solar shade on it.

02:12:55.170 --> 02:12:56.330
It's an amazing building.

02:12:56.330 --> 02:12:59.220
These are ceramic tubes
around the entire thing.

02:12:59.220 --> 02:13:02.750
It's got a veil to temper that
solar heat gain, still entirely

02:13:02.750 --> 02:13:07.970
glass building, and this is the
Hearst building, 57th Street

02:13:07.970 --> 02:13:10.600
and Eighth, I think.

02:13:10.600 --> 02:13:14.330
And that, again, has
very sophisticated

02:13:14.330 --> 02:13:19.130
specially selected glass
as part of its wall system.

02:13:21.780 --> 02:13:24.540
Also there are very
exotic materials

02:13:24.540 --> 02:13:28.210
that we really do need
to spend some effort on,

02:13:28.210 --> 02:13:31.170
and these are things Ike
phase change materials--

02:13:31.170 --> 02:13:37.200
so high thermal matured
materials that absorb or are

02:13:37.200 --> 02:13:39.720
give off heat as
they change state.

02:13:39.720 --> 02:13:41.950
These are extremely
useful materials.

02:13:41.950 --> 02:13:45.120
All of your seats could have
phase change materials in them,

02:13:45.120 --> 02:13:47.260
and we could lower the
temperature of this room

02:13:47.260 --> 02:13:51.240
all right so because the thermal
exchange would be at the user.

02:13:51.240 --> 02:13:53.790
Oops, sorry-- at the
user where it matters,

02:13:53.790 --> 02:13:56.490
not up there in
that volume where

02:13:56.490 --> 02:13:58.110
it doesn't matter as much.

02:13:58.110 --> 02:14:02.040
Solid state lighting is
a huge important field

02:14:02.040 --> 02:14:05.190
to reduce that artificial
lighting because there

02:14:05.190 --> 02:14:09.660
is a paper that is
published March 30, 2007,

02:14:09.660 --> 02:14:11.460
so it's fairly recent.

02:14:11.460 --> 02:14:14.250
It's on the materials for
aesthetic, energy efficient,

02:14:14.250 --> 02:14:15.990
and self-diagnostic buildings.

02:14:18.650 --> 02:14:20.450
But in addition to
materials, we also

02:14:20.450 --> 02:14:23.540
need some decision-making
tools for the designer.

02:14:23.540 --> 02:14:26.720
This is a material
selector software.

02:14:26.720 --> 02:14:29.450
The mechanical engineers have
had material selection software

02:14:29.450 --> 02:14:31.610
for a few decades now.

02:14:31.610 --> 02:14:34.160
The aero-astro certainly
have and the architects

02:14:34.160 --> 02:14:37.490
and the engineers, the stewards
of 70% of that material

02:14:37.490 --> 02:14:38.870
throughput have not.

02:14:38.870 --> 02:14:42.470
So with Mike Ashby at the
University of Cambridge

02:14:42.470 --> 02:14:46.800
in the UK, he and I developed
this as a tool, an initial tool

02:14:46.800 --> 02:14:49.430
to give architects
an idea of how

02:14:49.430 --> 02:14:52.520
to make rational decisions
between materials

02:14:52.520 --> 02:14:53.570
that they're considering.

02:14:53.570 --> 02:14:56.190
This is just actually showing
you that production energy

02:14:56.190 --> 02:14:58.100
or embodied energy,
which you can't read,

02:14:58.100 --> 02:15:01.240
versus CO2 emission
very tightly correlated.

02:15:01.240 --> 02:15:03.860
So a body then of CO2 material
and CO2 that's emitted.

02:15:03.860 --> 02:15:09.400
That's a tightly correlated
pairing of properties.

02:15:09.400 --> 02:15:12.460
And I haven't talked about
the production of power

02:15:12.460 --> 02:15:15.430
in buildings-- so building
integrated renewable

02:15:15.430 --> 02:15:16.990
energies, photovoltaics.

02:15:16.990 --> 02:15:18.790
I'm not even showing
photovoltaics.

02:15:18.790 --> 02:15:21.090
Photovoltaics is a
whole 'nother topic,

02:15:21.090 --> 02:15:23.200
and I think it is
extraordinarily important

02:15:23.200 --> 02:15:26.650
and will be part of the
solution for moderating demand.

02:15:26.650 --> 02:15:31.540
But I also wanted to show you
some maybe not so commonly seen

02:15:31.540 --> 02:15:32.860
images of a building.

02:15:32.860 --> 02:15:35.020
Actually, these are
renderings of a building

02:15:35.020 --> 02:15:36.730
in Chicago recently built--

02:15:36.730 --> 02:15:41.800
I think completed by
the previously big

02:15:41.800 --> 02:15:44.200
corporate architect Helmut Jahn.

02:15:44.200 --> 02:15:46.810
And that's a micro
turbine on the top

02:15:46.810 --> 02:15:48.930
of it runs the length
of the building

02:15:48.930 --> 02:15:52.720
and purportedly is supposed
to be delivering the lighting

02:15:52.720 --> 02:15:58.260
energy for low-income
housing, by the way.

02:15:58.260 --> 02:16:04.200
Okay, so the huge
elephant in the room

02:16:04.200 --> 02:16:07.500
is global climate change, which
I haven't really spoken about.

02:16:07.500 --> 02:16:09.600
I'll just spend just
a couple of slides.

02:16:09.600 --> 02:16:10.370
It's 51.

02:16:10.370 --> 02:16:13.890
I have like three minutes.

02:16:13.890 --> 02:16:17.340
there's

02:16:17.340 --> 02:16:20.940
A topic that I'd like to
introduce, not get into today

02:16:20.940 --> 02:16:25.950
but tell you that it is the
umbrella topic for efficiency.

02:16:25.950 --> 02:16:28.650
As you see an image of Hurricane
Maria heading for New Orleans.

02:16:31.350 --> 02:16:33.420
This talk of efficiency
gains is really

02:16:33.420 --> 02:16:36.049
set under the uncertainty
of vast changes

02:16:36.049 --> 02:16:38.219
to ecological
anthropogenic systems

02:16:38.219 --> 02:16:39.858
due to global climate change.

02:16:39.858 --> 02:16:42.150
If energy efficiencies brought
to the built environment

02:16:42.150 --> 02:16:44.317
do not account for the
uncertainty of global climate

02:16:44.317 --> 02:16:47.261
change, we run the risk
of being overwhelmed

02:16:47.261 --> 02:16:48.719
by producing
buildings that rapidly

02:16:48.719 --> 02:16:51.450
become inappropriate
in their locations

02:16:51.450 --> 02:16:53.100
and for their occupants.

02:16:53.100 --> 02:16:55.743
An emerging set
of priorities then

02:16:55.743 --> 02:16:57.660
is starting to really
change the way a growing

02:16:57.660 --> 02:16:58.980
group of designers--

02:16:58.980 --> 02:17:00.650
I already mentioned
this-- designers,

02:17:00.650 --> 02:17:03.090
industrial ecologists,
engineers, planners,

02:17:03.090 --> 02:17:06.480
urban planners are thinking
about the new future city,

02:17:06.480 --> 02:17:08.700
the green city.

02:17:08.700 --> 02:17:10.860
That is, if we can accept
the notion that the built

02:17:10.860 --> 02:17:13.559
environment is one very
large complex system,

02:17:13.559 --> 02:17:15.270
then it would be
prudent to explore

02:17:15.270 --> 02:17:17.459
the ways in which that
system can be designed

02:17:17.459 --> 02:17:22.580
within an enhanced capacity
for adaptation and resilience

02:17:22.580 --> 02:17:25.780
and ability to respond
to shocks to the system.

02:17:25.780 --> 02:17:31.230
So I'll leave you just
with two current designs

02:17:31.230 --> 02:17:32.910
that I'm working on.

02:17:32.910 --> 02:17:35.980
One is a kind of
a modest proposal.

02:17:35.980 --> 02:17:38.010
It's for a house.

02:17:38.010 --> 02:17:39.690
We're calling it
the MIT float house.

02:17:39.690 --> 02:17:43.049
I am privileged to be involved
in a number of projects

02:17:43.049 --> 02:17:44.063
in New Orleans.

02:17:44.063 --> 02:17:45.480
Believe me, the
next two years are

02:17:45.480 --> 02:17:47.940
going to be really
interesting in New Orleans.

02:17:47.940 --> 02:17:50.639
A lot is happening, not only
just because of the hurricanes

02:17:50.639 --> 02:17:51.980
but a lot of them.

02:17:51.980 --> 02:17:53.730
You go to any restaurant
there, and no one

02:17:53.730 --> 02:17:55.799
in the room, no one
in the restaurant

02:17:55.799 --> 02:17:57.250
had been there pre-Katrina.

02:17:57.250 --> 02:17:59.370
And they're all PhDs and
engineers, and I mean,

02:17:59.370 --> 02:18:01.889
it's an interesting place.

02:18:01.889 --> 02:18:03.940
This house is designed--

02:18:03.940 --> 02:18:07.080
it proposes an alternative
to relying on a centralized

02:18:07.080 --> 02:18:09.540
levee-based flood
protection system--

02:18:09.540 --> 02:18:10.257
very simple.

02:18:10.257 --> 02:18:11.840
The house floats
when the city floods.

02:18:15.910 --> 02:18:18.730
The city will flood.

02:18:18.730 --> 02:18:21.670
People are building now
houses slab on grade--

02:18:21.670 --> 02:18:25.719
in other words, the concrete
at grade, building up,

02:18:25.719 --> 02:18:26.670
and it will flood.

02:18:26.670 --> 02:18:29.590
So okay, so but the
implication here

02:18:29.590 --> 02:18:31.990
is it goes far beyond
just this design.

02:18:31.990 --> 02:18:35.350
And that is, is
there an opportunity

02:18:35.350 --> 02:18:38.075
to rethink the way we actually
deploy infrastructure.

02:18:38.075 --> 02:18:39.700
And we actually design
architecture not

02:18:39.700 --> 02:18:43.090
to depend on these large
centralized systems,

02:18:43.090 --> 02:18:44.750
even for power generation.

02:18:44.750 --> 02:18:48.610
For example, what if we were to
develop ways in which we could

02:18:48.610 --> 02:18:52.500
distribute using microgrids
renewable energy community

02:18:52.500 --> 02:18:57.280
organized and owned systems
for power generation?

02:18:57.280 --> 02:19:04.010
That would simultaneously
lead to a reduction in demand.

02:19:04.010 --> 02:19:07.930
And anyone that owns the
thing that's producing energy

02:19:07.930 --> 02:19:09.320
is going to maintain it.

02:19:09.320 --> 02:19:11.778
They're going to want to know
how much energy [INAUDIBLE]..

02:19:11.778 --> 02:19:14.530
They have the knowledge of what
it is that they're getting at.

02:19:14.530 --> 02:19:16.988
You just write a check to the
energy company for that power

02:19:16.988 --> 02:19:18.309
plant, which is 70 miles away.

02:19:18.309 --> 02:19:20.142
Well, there's kind of
a different engagement

02:19:20.142 --> 02:19:22.010
dynamic going on there.

02:19:22.010 --> 02:19:25.900
So but it also then says
that that community could

02:19:25.900 --> 02:19:27.360
be more resilient to shocks.

02:19:27.360 --> 02:19:29.889
So when it does
flood, this house

02:19:29.889 --> 02:19:33.760
elevates because it's got a
chassis of a buoyant material.

02:19:33.760 --> 02:19:35.320
It's held in place
because you've

02:19:35.320 --> 02:19:42.840
got paragraphs, which that
make for a purely vertical rise

02:19:42.840 --> 02:19:44.200
and keep it in place.

02:19:44.200 --> 02:19:45.660
So it deals with
lateral movement.

02:19:45.660 --> 02:19:49.040
And these hydraulic
columns fill with water.

02:19:49.040 --> 02:19:51.557
So these fill with water.

02:19:51.557 --> 02:19:52.390
The flood goes away.

02:19:52.390 --> 02:19:53.060
Water goes away.

02:19:53.060 --> 02:19:55.120
This is not a fiction.

02:19:55.120 --> 02:19:56.300
The floodwaters are gone.

02:19:56.300 --> 02:19:59.050
The house is still up because
the thing you also want to do

02:19:59.050 --> 02:20:01.120
is to protect the
house from looting.

02:20:01.120 --> 02:20:04.150
So it's a lot harder to
loot this house then.

02:20:04.150 --> 02:20:07.360
Okay, so the owner comes back,
or the community comes back

02:20:07.360 --> 02:20:10.780
and slowly lowers each one.

02:20:10.780 --> 02:20:13.810
The second proposal, I
actually want to talk about--

02:20:13.810 --> 02:20:14.440
I'm a designer.

02:20:14.440 --> 02:20:15.340
I throw out these ideas.

02:20:15.340 --> 02:20:16.270
Let's see what sticks.

02:20:20.997 --> 02:20:22.330
Both of these are real projects.

02:20:22.330 --> 02:20:26.300
I'm talking to a developer on
the house I showed you before.

02:20:26.300 --> 02:20:28.240
And this project
is also in play,

02:20:28.240 --> 02:20:32.440
and that is the idea which I
just mentioned of a zero-energy

02:20:32.440 --> 02:20:36.940
community that has an
emergency water system,

02:20:36.940 --> 02:20:41.050
on-site storm retention basins,
thermal storage reservoir,

02:20:41.050 --> 02:20:43.750
phase change materials that
can circulate throughout all

02:20:43.750 --> 02:20:46.240
the buildings,
microwind turbines,

02:20:46.240 --> 02:20:48.210
building integrated
photovoltaics,

02:20:48.210 --> 02:20:51.340
and emergency power source
served by the micro grid

02:20:51.340 --> 02:20:53.680
and local flood surge break--

02:20:53.680 --> 02:20:57.080
all within the goal of a
diminished carbon footprint.

02:20:57.080 --> 02:21:01.180
This is the idea of a resilient
semi-autonomous community

02:21:01.180 --> 02:21:03.810
that is now green.

02:21:03.810 --> 02:21:07.050
So these are some ideas that
I'm working on recently.

02:21:07.050 --> 02:21:09.140
I think this is a
direction that--

02:21:09.140 --> 02:21:11.690
at least for me in my work
in building technologies--

02:21:11.690 --> 02:21:13.910
is where I think
we can contribute

02:21:13.910 --> 02:21:15.480
to moderating demand.

02:21:15.480 --> 02:21:18.440
Let me just read my
last paragraph here.

02:21:18.440 --> 02:21:20.660
The idea that resilience
and adaptability

02:21:20.660 --> 02:21:23.780
can be design priorities
initiates a whole new era

02:21:23.780 --> 02:21:27.740
for architecture that
incorporates as central core

02:21:27.740 --> 02:21:30.200
priorities are
evolving understanding

02:21:30.200 --> 02:21:33.850
of global resource consumption
and global climate exchange.

02:21:33.850 --> 02:21:35.980
It will certainly be a
challenge to fully assume

02:21:35.980 --> 02:21:37.830
this approach within
a business that

02:21:37.830 --> 02:21:41.000
has always resisted any change.

02:21:41.000 --> 02:21:43.210
But I'm quite optimistic
that real change,

02:21:43.210 --> 02:21:46.883
productive and
positive, is coming.

02:21:46.883 --> 02:21:48.800
I think it can be a
critical part of that too.

02:21:48.800 --> 02:21:53.820
There's an enormous
wealth of momentum here.

02:21:53.820 --> 02:21:57.750
I'm not entirely counting
on political momentum,

02:21:57.750 --> 02:22:02.700
political will, counting on
a lot of other kinds of will.

02:22:02.700 --> 02:22:08.240
So if you hire an architect,
as opposed to what Bucky Fuller

02:22:08.240 --> 02:22:11.752
asked the architect, how much
does your wall building weigh

02:22:11.752 --> 02:22:14.210
years and years ago-- though
it's a decent question to ask,

02:22:14.210 --> 02:22:16.100
by the way--

02:22:16.100 --> 02:22:17.660
you should ask
your architect, how

02:22:17.660 --> 02:22:22.190
is the person or firm going to
produce a strategy for reducing

02:22:22.190 --> 02:22:25.940
the resource consumption of
that architectural proposal.

02:22:25.940 --> 02:22:27.950
I hope personally that
the answer that you get

02:22:27.950 --> 02:22:29.600
comes from one of
my former students

02:22:29.600 --> 02:22:32.102
because then I can
assured that it can be

02:22:32.102 --> 02:22:33.578
both effective and beautiful.

02:22:33.578 --> 02:22:35.054
Thank you.

02:22:35.054 --> 02:22:43.910
[APPLAUSE]

02:22:50.830 --> 02:22:54.045
So I can take five minutes
worth of questions.

02:22:56.775 --> 02:22:59.140
Yes.

02:22:59.140 --> 02:23:03.620
AUDIENCE: First, I'd like to say
that I was really disappointed

02:23:03.620 --> 02:23:05.300
up until your talk
because I thought

02:23:05.300 --> 02:23:07.790
I was at the other
place down the river

02:23:07.790 --> 02:23:11.780
because it was all blabber
and very little reality.

02:23:11.780 --> 02:23:14.240
And you brought a lot of
reality to this thing.

02:23:14.240 --> 02:23:15.620
No, I mean,
seriously because you

02:23:15.620 --> 02:23:18.170
can do all kinds of studies
and projections and everything,

02:23:18.170 --> 02:23:19.850
but you're building real things.

02:23:19.850 --> 02:23:22.040
And you're dealing
with real things.

02:23:22.040 --> 02:23:25.910
And that's very important
because if we knew the answers,

02:23:25.910 --> 02:23:29.210
we'd be at Foxwoods, not here.

02:23:29.210 --> 02:23:31.220
FERNANDEZ: Can I
respond, actually?

02:23:31.220 --> 02:23:32.970
AUDIENCE: It's a
preamble to the question.

02:23:32.970 --> 02:23:37.010
FERNANDEZ: Yeah, but I did say
that my piece was one of four

02:23:37.010 --> 02:23:38.960
was moderating demand.

02:23:38.960 --> 02:23:40.670
My opinion is
moderating demand will

02:23:40.670 --> 02:23:45.020
be an extraordinarily small
portion of what will contribute

02:23:45.020 --> 02:23:49.760
to the stabilization of CO2
levels in the atmosphere

02:23:49.760 --> 02:23:50.650
and all that.

02:23:50.650 --> 02:23:52.448
So that's one really small part.

02:23:52.448 --> 02:23:54.990
All the other abstract work and
large infrastructure systems,

02:23:54.990 --> 02:23:55.490
all that--

02:23:55.490 --> 02:24:00.290
I am totally on board
with all of that

02:24:00.290 --> 02:24:01.970
having to be talked
about in the way

02:24:01.970 --> 02:24:03.845
that Professor Deutsch
talked about it, which

02:24:03.845 --> 02:24:07.138
is that if I say it's
possible, somebody doesn't say,

02:24:07.138 --> 02:24:08.180
oh, I can do it tomorrow.

02:24:08.180 --> 02:24:09.930
But we need to figure
out how to do it.

02:24:09.930 --> 02:24:14.180
AUDIENCE: Well, first of all,
as the head of NASA said,

02:24:14.180 --> 02:24:17.900
why are we saying that this is
the climate that we necessarily

02:24:17.900 --> 02:24:19.550
want to maintain?

02:24:19.550 --> 02:24:22.790
Why don't we just
see perhaps that we

02:24:22.790 --> 02:24:24.832
can adapt to the climate
as we have always

02:24:24.832 --> 02:24:25.790
adapted to the climate?

02:24:25.790 --> 02:24:29.400
When it got cold, we put our
clothing on, built shelter.

02:24:29.400 --> 02:24:32.858
When it got warm, we moved,
whatever the case may be.

02:24:32.858 --> 02:24:34.400
So the point is,
and the second thing

02:24:34.400 --> 02:24:39.920
is it's really, really bad
for people in a place like MIT

02:24:39.920 --> 02:24:43.640
to deny reality in the sense
that they say CO2 is the most

02:24:43.640 --> 02:24:44.930
important greenhouse gas.

02:24:44.930 --> 02:24:45.280
FERNANDEZ: I'm sorry.

02:24:45.280 --> 02:24:46.238
Do you have a question?

02:24:46.238 --> 02:24:49.850
AUDIENCE: Yes, any
gas with those number

02:24:49.850 --> 02:24:52.950
of atoms that present
will do essentially,

02:24:52.950 --> 02:24:54.620
the same thing in
water vapor, which

02:24:54.620 --> 02:24:57.380
is far more prevalent
in the atmosphere

02:24:57.380 --> 02:24:59.930
is much more important
as a greenhouse gas.

02:24:59.930 --> 02:25:01.590
But let's leave that out there.

02:25:01.590 --> 02:25:03.260
The question is this.

02:25:03.260 --> 02:25:08.190
If you foresee the global
drive going the way it is--

02:25:08.190 --> 02:25:10.730
and that's, again,
a projection--

02:25:10.730 --> 02:25:14.390
with decreasing population
growth rates in the developed

02:25:14.390 --> 02:25:18.030
countries, how do
we take advantage

02:25:18.030 --> 02:25:21.780
of our expertise
and our knowledge

02:25:21.780 --> 02:25:24.780
in the built
environment and transfer

02:25:24.780 --> 02:25:27.810
that in a productive
way to the third world,

02:25:27.810 --> 02:25:31.260
fourth world whatever where the
big growth is going to occur.

02:25:31.260 --> 02:25:32.700
How do we do it productive?

02:25:32.700 --> 02:25:35.730
FERNANDEZ: So there is a
very, very quick answer

02:25:35.730 --> 02:25:39.660
because I want to get one
other question in here.

02:25:39.660 --> 02:25:41.910
The answer to your question
is that is happening.

02:25:41.910 --> 02:25:44.550
There are literally
hundreds of people

02:25:44.550 --> 02:25:46.080
thinking exactly
about that question

02:25:46.080 --> 02:25:46.920
and doing different things.

02:25:46.920 --> 02:25:49.490
For example one quick example
is LEAD in the United States.

02:25:49.490 --> 02:25:53.550
So you know LEAD is Leadership
in Energy and Environmental

02:25:53.550 --> 02:25:54.525
Design.

02:25:54.525 --> 02:25:56.240
It'd build a rating system--

02:25:56.240 --> 02:25:57.240
I think that's correct--

02:25:57.240 --> 02:26:00.720
build a rating system
that is intended

02:26:00.720 --> 02:26:03.810
to be used by architects
to certify that they've

02:26:03.810 --> 02:26:06.760
done as much as they
can to reduce resource

02:26:06.760 --> 02:26:08.010
consumption of their building.

02:26:08.010 --> 02:26:08.885
It's a rating system.

02:26:08.885 --> 02:26:10.650
It's not an actual
monitoring of that,

02:26:10.650 --> 02:26:13.890
but it is a tool that's now
being transferred to countries

02:26:13.890 --> 02:26:15.380
all over the world
or the attempt

02:26:15.380 --> 02:26:17.460
is to transfer it to
countries all over the world

02:26:17.460 --> 02:26:19.870
so that at least there's a
benchmark that architects can

02:26:19.870 --> 02:26:20.370
work with.

02:26:20.370 --> 02:26:22.520
So that's just very, very quick.

02:26:22.520 --> 02:26:27.353
But rest assured there's
very good awareness.

02:26:27.353 --> 02:26:29.145
And there's huge effort
to transfer what we

02:26:29.145 --> 02:26:30.420
know to the developing world.

02:26:30.420 --> 02:26:32.130
And by the way, the
developing world

02:26:32.130 --> 02:26:34.830
is more politically
progressive than many

02:26:34.830 --> 02:26:37.560
of the countries in
the developed world,

02:26:37.560 --> 02:26:39.080
I think might be.

02:26:39.080 --> 02:26:41.880
The one last question.

02:26:41.880 --> 02:26:44.490
AUDIENCE: Is there any
percentage of reduction

02:26:44.490 --> 02:26:47.910
of energy consumption from
simply planting deciduous

02:26:47.910 --> 02:26:51.390
trees like on the southwest,
east sides of the home?

02:26:51.390 --> 02:26:54.040
FERNANDEZ: Yeah, great question.

02:26:54.040 --> 02:26:55.980
And that is to hit
solar heat gain.

02:26:55.980 --> 02:26:59.050
So when you reduce
solar heat gain--

02:26:59.050 --> 02:27:00.960
and it varies tremendously
with climate--

02:27:00.960 --> 02:27:02.760
but in a climate
like this, solar heat

02:27:02.760 --> 02:27:05.520
gain on the west side, for
example, late afternoon,

02:27:05.520 --> 02:27:08.392
the building's already warm.

02:27:08.392 --> 02:27:09.850
I'm not going to
give you a number,

02:27:09.850 --> 02:27:11.558
but there is significant
savings that you

02:27:11.558 --> 02:27:14.730
can have by making sure
that that building does not

02:27:14.730 --> 02:27:16.620
heat up late in the
afternoon, and you

02:27:16.620 --> 02:27:20.190
continue to need some
kind of air conditioning.

02:27:20.190 --> 02:27:21.260
But that's a good point.

02:27:21.260 --> 02:27:25.830
Okay, actually, the
point is being stressed

02:27:25.830 --> 02:27:27.060
that lunch is on the tables.

02:27:27.060 --> 02:27:28.560
Thank you.

02:27:28.560 --> 02:27:30.660
Enjoy, thank you.

02:27:30.660 --> 02:27:34.310
[APPLAUSE]

